// Flashcards rebuilt 4 Nov 2026 from the Google Doc "NMUN 2027" and trimmed to the essentials: [unitId, front, back]. Card IDs derive from unit id + front text.
window.CARDS = [
  ["lon","Which of Wilson's Fourteen Points led to the League, and when did he present them?","The last one (Point 14). Presented to Congress on 8 January 1918. It called for \"a general association of nations\"."],
  ["lon","League of Nations: founding document?","The Covenant, Part I of the Treaty of Versailles (signed 28 June 1919 at the Paris Peace Conference)."],
  ["lon","When did the League start, with how many founding members, and where was it based?","10 January 1920, 42 founding members, Geneva."],
  ["lon","Why did the US never join the League?","The Senate refused to ratify Versailles, fearing entanglement in foreign wars (isolationism)."],
  ["lon","League of Nations: which great powers sat on its Council, and what about Germany and the USSR?","• Permanent Council members of the League at its founding: UK, France, Italy, Japan.\n• Germany and the USSR in the League: Germany joined 1926, left 1933. USSR joined 1934, expelled 1939 for invading Finland."],
  ["lon","League membership numbers?","Peak of 58 states (1934–35). 63 states were members at some point."],
  ["lon","Which state was the last to join the League?","Egypt, in 1937."],
  ["lon","How did the League end?","It dissolved itself at its final Assembly in April 1946, transferring assets to the UN."],
  ["lon","Why did the League of Nations fail?","The unanimity rule, missing great powers, no enforcement, failed test cases (Manchuria, Abyssinia) and appeasement."],
  ["lon","What happened in Manchuria (1931)?","Japan invaded. The Lytton Report took a year and condemned Japan, which simply left the League in 1933."],
  ["lon","What happened in Abyssinia (1935–36)?","Italy invaded Ethiopia. Sanctions excluded oil, and Britain and France kept the Suez Canal open, so they failed."],
  ["lon","Appeasement: what happened after the Munich Agreement (1938)?","Germany took the Sudetenland, then invaded the rest of Czechoslovakia (March 1939) and Poland (1 September 1939). War broke out."],
  ["lon","Four League weaknesses: what was the UN fix for each?","• League weakness: unanimity paralysis. What was the UN fix: Majority voting everywhere. Unanimity only among the P5 on substantive Security Council votes (the veto).\n• League weakness: no enforcement. What was the UN fix: Chapter VII: binding sanctions and authorised force; later peacekeeping.\n• League weakness: great powers absent. What was the UN fix: Permanent seats and a veto for the great powers, so they stay inside.\n• League weakness: no ban on war. What was the UN fix: Article 2(4) prohibits the threat or use of force."],
  ["lon","Which League bodies lived on in the UN system?","The ILO (1919) became the first specialized agency. The Permanent Court of International Justice became the ICJ. The mandates system became the Trusteeship system."],
  ["lon","Two League successes worth one line?","The Åland Islands dispute (1921) and the Nansen passport for refugees."],
  ["lon","When and where was the WWI armistice signed?","11 November 1918, in a railway carriage in the forest of Compiègne, France."],
  ["lon","How did the US Senate block US membership?","It rejected Versailles twice (November 1919, March 1920). Republicans led by Henry Cabot Lodge feared Article 10 would drag the US into wars without Congress's approval. Wilson, weakened by a stroke, refused to compromise."],
  ["lon","What was Article 10 of the Covenant?","Collective defence of members' territory. It was the Senate's main objection."],
  ["lon","League Assembly: who sat in it, how often did it meet, and what was its UN successor?","All members, one vote each, met yearly in Geneva. Successor: the General Assembly."],
  ["lon","League Council: who sat in it, what did it do, and what was its UN successor?","Permanent members (UK, France, Italy, Japan; later Germany and the USSR) plus elected members; handled disputes. Successor: the Security Council."],
  ["lon","Who was the League's first Secretary-General?","Sir Eric Drummond (UK). UN successor body: the Secretariat."],
  ["lon","Permanent Court of International Justice: when, where, what did it do, and what replaced it?","Founded 1922 in The Hague. Settled disputes between states and gave advisory opinions. Replaced by the ICJ."],
  ["lon","League Mandates Commission: what did it do and what replaced it?","Supervised former German and Ottoman territories. Replaced by the Trusteeship Council."],
  ["lon","League Health Organisation and Nansen Office: what did they do and what replaced them?","Epidemics and refugee passports. Successors: WHO and UNHCR."],
  ["lon","League vs UN: how do they differ (creation, decisions, war, enforcement, scope, membership)?","• League vs UN: how was each created: League: after WWI, by the victors. UN: planned during WWII, drafted by 50 states at San Francisco.\n• League vs UN: decision-making: League: unanimity, so every member had a de facto veto. UN: majority voting, with a veto only for the P5 on substantive Security Council votes.\n• League vs UN: ban on war: League: delayed war with cooling-off periods, no outright ban. UN: Article 2(4) bans force; exceptions only for self-defence and Security Council authorisation.\n• League vs UN: enforcement: League: no forces; sanctions depended on willing members. UN: Chapter VII binding sanctions and force, plus peacekeeping.\n• League vs UN: scope: League: mainly security, some social work. UN: security plus development, human rights and humanitarian work (ECOSOC, agencies).\n• League vs UN: peak membership: League: 58 states. UN: 193 states."],
  ["lon","In 30 seconds: why did the League fail and how did the UN fix it?","The League needed unanimity, had no enforcement and lacked the great powers (no US, others left). It failed over Manchuria, Abyssinia and appeasement. The UN fixed this with majority voting, Chapter VII enforcement, permanent seats with a veto for the great powers, and Article 2(4)."],
  ["founding","How was the UN designed, compared with the League?","During the Second World War by the Allies. The League was built after the First World War."],
  ["founding","12 June 1941?","Declaration of St James's Palace (London). The Allies pledge to work for a peace based on cooperation."],
  ["founding","14 August 1941?","The Atlantic Charter (Roosevelt and Churchill, at sea off Newfoundland). Principles for the post-war world: self-determination, no territorial gains, collective security."],
  ["founding","1 January 1942?","Declaration by United Nations, signed by 26 states. First official use of the name \"United Nations\", coined by Roosevelt."],
  ["founding","30 October 1943?","Moscow Declaration (US, UK, USSR, China). It commits to a new \"general international organization\"."],
  ["founding","November–December 1943?","Tehran Conference. Roosevelt, Churchill and Stalin meet; the post-war organization is discussed."],
  ["founding","August–October 1944?","Dumbarton Oaks Conference (Washington, DC). First blueprint: General Assembly, Security Council, Court, Secretariat. Voting in the Council left open."],
  ["founding","February 1945?","Yalta Conference. Agrees the Security Council voting formula: the P5 veto."],
  ["founding","25 April–26 June 1945?","San Francisco Conference (UNCIO), 50 states. The Charter was drafted and signed on 26 June 1945."],
  ["founding","24 October 1945?","The Charter enters into force, after ratification by the P5 and a majority of signatories. Now UN Day."],
  ["founding","10 January 1946?","First General Assembly, Methodist Central Hall, London. 51 members."],
  ["founding","17 January 1946?","First Security Council meeting, London."],
  ["founding","1 February 1946?","Trygve Lie (Norway) appointed first Secretary-General. Gladwyn Jebb (UK) had been acting Executive Secretary."],
  ["founding","Walk through the road to the UN in order.","1941: St James's Palace and Atlantic Charter. 1942: Declaration by United Nations. 1943: Moscow Declaration and Tehran. 1944: Dumbarton Oaks. 1945: Yalta, San Francisco, Charter in force on 24 October. 1946: first GA and Council, Lie appointed."],
  ["founding","Why are there 51 original members when 50 states signed at San Francisco?","Poland signed later (its government was not settled in time) and still counts as an original member."],
  ["founding","Where is UN headquarters, and who donated the land?","• Where is the UN's permanent headquarters, and who donated the land: New York. Land donated by John D. Rockefeller Jr. Completed 1952.\n• Who paid for the Manhattan headquarters site, and how much: John D. Rockefeller Jr., $8.5 million, East River site (December 1946)."],
  ["founding","Who were the \"Four Policemen\", and why does France have a permanent seat?","• Why was France not one of the \"Four Policemen\", yet has a permanent seat: It was occupied during the war. It was given a permanent seat at San Francisco.\n• Who were the \"Four Policemen\": US, UK, USSR, China."],
  ["founding","Charter signed vs in force: what are the dates, and what is the common mistake?","Signed 26 June 1945. In force 24 October 1945. Saying it was \"ratified on 24 October\" is a common mistake."],
  ["founding","Atlantic Charter: where was it agreed, and what were its eight points?","• Where was the Atlantic Charter agreed, and was the US at war: Aboard warships in Placentia Bay, Newfoundland. The US was not yet at war.\n• Atlantic Charter: the eight points: No territorial gains. No territorial changes against the people's wishes. Self-determination. Equal access to trade and raw materials. Economic cooperation. Freedom from fear and want. Freedom of the seas. Abandoning force, with disarmament of aggressors."],
  ["founding","Declaration by United Nations: where was it signed, by whom, and what did they pledge?","Washington, 26 states led by the US, UK, USSR and China. They pledged to use full resources against the Axis, not to make a separate peace, and to uphold the Atlantic Charter."],
  ["founding","Moscow Conference, October 1943: who attended and what came out of it?","Foreign ministers of the US, UK and USSR, with China joining for one text. Four declarations: General Security, Italy, Austria, Atrocities."],
  ["founding","What was special about the Moscow Declaration on General Security?","The first commitment to a \"general international organization\" for the maintenance of international peace and security."],
  ["founding","Dumbarton Oaks: where and when?","A mansion in Washington, 21 August–7 October 1944."],
  ["founding","Which bodies did the Dumbarton Oaks Proposals create?","A General Assembly with an Economic and Social Council under it, a Security Council, a court and a Secretariat."],
  ["founding","What did the Dumbarton Oaks Proposals say about armed forces, and why?","Members would place armed forces at the Council's disposal, because the League's lack of force had been fatal."],
  ["founding","Yalta: where and when?","Crimea, 4–11 February 1945."],
  ["founding","What was the Yalta voting formula?","Unanimity of the permanent members on substantive matters, but not on procedure or when they are party to a dispute under Chapter VI."],
  ["founding","Yalta: what did it decide about the Soviet republics?","Two extra seats for the USSR. Ukraine and Byelorussia became original members."],
  ["founding","UNCIO: dates, size, and when it opened relative to Roosevelt's death?","25 April–26 June 1945, 50 states, about 850 delegates. It opened two weeks after Roosevelt's death."],
  ["founding","What did smaller and middle powers win at San Francisco?","A stronger General Assembly, ECOSOC as a principal organ, the human rights language, regional arrangements (Chapter VIII) and collective self-defence in Article 51."],
  ["founding","What was signed on 26 June 1945?","The Charter and the ICJ Statute."],
  ["founding","What does Article 110 require for the Charter to enter into force?","Ratification by China, France, the USSR, the UK and the US, plus a majority of the other signatories. This was reached on 24 October 1945."],
  ["founding","First General Assembly: who presided, and what was its first resolution?","President Paul-Henri Spaak of Belgium. Its first resolution (January 1946) set up a commission on atomic energy."],
  ["founding","In 30 seconds: how was the UN created?","Wartime Allies built it step by step. The Atlantic Charter (1941) set the principles, the Declaration by United Nations (1942) coined the name, Dumbarton Oaks (1944) drafted the structure, and Yalta (1945) settled the veto. 50 states signed the Charter at San Francisco on 26 June 1945, and it came into force on 24 October 1945."],
  ["charter","What is the UN Charter, and how big is it?","The UN's founding treaty: 19 chapters, 111 articles, plus the annexed Statute of the ICJ (70 articles). It binds every member."],
  ["charter","List the four purposes of the UN (Article 1).","1. Maintain international peace and security. 2. Develop friendly relations based on equal rights and self-determination of peoples. 3. Achieve international cooperation on economic, social, cultural and humanitarian problems and promote human rights. 4. Be a centre for harmonizing the actions of nations."],
  ["charter","Charter Article 2: what do paragraphs 1, 3, 4 and 7 say?","• Article 2(1): Sovereign equality of all members.\n• Article 2(3): Settle disputes by peaceful means.\n• Article 2(4): Refrain from the threat or use of force against the territorial integrity or political independence of any state.\n• Article 2(7): The UN may not intervene in matters essentially within a state's domestic jurisdiction, except enforcement measures under Chapter VII."],
  ["charter","Charter chapters I–VI: what does each cover?","• Charter Chapters I to III: I (Arts 1–2): Purposes and Principles. II (Arts 3–6): Membership (admission, suspension, expulsion). III (Arts 7–8): Organs.\n• Charter Chapter IV: Articles 9–22: the General Assembly.\n• Charter Chapter V: Articles 23–32: the Security Council.\n• Charter Chapter VI: Articles 33–38: Pacific Settlement of Disputes."],
  ["charter","Charter chapters VII–XI: what does each cover?","• Charter Chapter VII: Articles 39–51: Threats to the peace, breaches of the peace, acts of aggression.\n• Charter Chapter VIII: Articles 52–54: Regional Arrangements.\n• Charter Chapters IX and X: IX (Arts 55–60): International Economic and Social Cooperation. X (Arts 61–72): the Economic and Social Council.\n• Charter Chapter XI: Articles 73–74: Non-Self-Governing Territories."],
  ["charter","Charter chapters XII–XIX: what does each cover?","• Charter Chapters XII and XIII: XII (Arts 75–85): International Trusteeship System. XIII (Arts 86–91): Trusteeship Council.\n• Charter Chapter XIV: Articles 92–96: the International Court of Justice.\n• Charter Chapter XV: Articles 97–101: the Secretariat.\n• Charter Chapter XVI: Articles 102–105: Miscellaneous (treaty registration, Charter supremacy).\n• Charter Chapters XVII to XIX: XVII (Arts 106–107): Transitional Security Arrangements. XVIII (Arts 108–109): Amendments. XIX (Arts 110–111): Ratification and Signature."],
  ["charter","Which chapters and articles cover the Security Council, the General Assembly and the ICJ?","Security Council: Chapter V, Arts 23–32. General Assembly: Chapter IV, Arts 9–22. ICJ: Chapter XIV, Arts 92–96."],
  ["charter","Which chapters deal with disputes and with threats to the peace?","Chapter VI (Arts 33–38): peaceful settlement. Chapter VII (Arts 39–51): threats, breaches and aggression."],
  ["charter","Charter amendments: how many, which rounds, why, and the last one?","• How many times has the Charter been amended: Five amendments, adopted in three rounds (in force 1965, 1968, 1973). None since 1973.\n• Charter amendments in force in 1965: Art. 23 enlarged the Security Council from 11 to 15. Art. 27 raised the voting threshold from 7 to 9. Art. 61 enlarged ECOSOC from 18 to 27.\n• Charter amendment in force in 1968: Art. 109 adjusted to the larger Council: a review conference can be called by any nine SC members.\n• Charter amendment in force in 1973: Art. 61 enlarged ECOSOC from 27 to 54.\n• Why were all the Charter amendments made: Decolonization: membership grew from 51 (1945) to 117 (1965) and 135 (1973), so the Security Council and ECOSOC had to grow to keep regional representation fair.\n• When was the Charter last amended: 1973. None since.\n• Which amendment round enlarged the Security Council: The 1965 round: Art. 23 (11 to 15) and Art. 27 (voting threshold 7 to 9)."],
  ["charter","Why is Article 23 outdated, and who holds the seats today?","It still says \"Republic of China\" and \"Union of Soviet Socialist Republics\". The PRC has held China's seat since 1971. The Russian Federation has held the USSR's since December 1991, as a continuator state accepted without a vote."],
  ["charter","Why does the Security Council have 15 members and ECOSOC 54?","Decolonization swelled UN membership, so amendments enlarged them (SC 11 to 15; ECOSOC 18 to 27 to 54) for fair regional representation."],
  ["charter","Charter authentic texts and official languages of the UN?","• Which languages are the Charter's authentic texts (Art. 111): Chinese, French, Russian, English, Spanish.\n• When did Arabic become an official language, and how many official languages are there today: Arabic became an official language of the GA in 1973, making six today."],
  ["charter","Which chapter covers Charter supremacy and treaty registration?","Chapter XVI (Arts 102–105), Miscellaneous."],
  ["charter","Which chapter covers regional organisations such as the African Union?","Chapter VIII (Arts 52–54), Regional Arrangements."],
  ["charter","Where does the Charter deal with suspension and expulsion of members?","Chapter II, Arts 3–6."],
  ["charter","Which chapter and articles cover non-self-governing territories?","Chapter XI, Arts 73–74."],
  ["charter","Which chapters cover trusteeship?","XII sets up the Trusteeship System (Arts 75–85); XIII covers the Trusteeship Council (Arts 86–91)."],
  ["charter","In 30 seconds: what is the Charter and what are its core rules?","The UN's founding treaty (19 chapters, 111 articles). Purposes: peace and security, friendly relations, cooperation and human rights, a centre for harmonizing nations. Core principles: sovereign equality, peaceful settlement, no threat or use of force (2(4)), no intervention in domestic matters (2(7)) except under Chapter VII."],
  ["charter","Which article sets the Security Council's voting threshold, and what is it?","Article 27. It was raised from 7 to 9 votes in 1965."],
  ["membership","How many UN member states are there, and which joined last?","193. South Sudan, 14 July 2011."],
  ["membership","Which two non-member observer states exist?","The Holy See (observer since 1964) and the State of Palestine (observer state since 2012)."],
  ["membership","How many member states recognize Palestine, and which Western states did so in September 2025?","Over 150. The UK, France, Canada and Australia recognized it in September 2025."],
  ["membership","What are the three steps to join the UN (Article 4)?","1. The state applies to the Secretary-General with a declaration accepting Charter obligations. 2. The Security Council recommends admission: 9 votes and no P5 veto. 3. The General Assembly admits it by a two-thirds majority."],
  ["membership","Can a P5 veto block a new member?","Yes. The Council's recommendation needs no P5 veto, so one veto blocks admission."],
  ["membership","Does the UN \"recognize\" states?","No. Only states recognize other states. The UN admits members and accepts the credentials of governments (checked by the GA's Credentials Committee)."],
  ["membership","Articles 5, 6 and 19: suspension, expulsion and arrears (how Article 19 is applied)?","• Article 5: Suspension of rights when the SC takes enforcement action against a member. Never formally used.\n• Article 6: Expulsion for persistent violation of Charter principles. Never used.\n• Article 19: Loss of GA vote for arrears of two full years. Applied regularly; states can get an exemption if non-payment is beyond their control.\n• How is Article 19 applied each year: The Secretary-General notifies the GA of members in arrears. The GA sometimes grants exemptions when non-payment is beyond a state's control."],
  ["membership","What happened to South Africa's delegation at the GA in 1974?","Barred over apartheid; returned in 1994. A credentials decision, not a suspension of membership."],
  ["membership","Yugoslavia and the UN in 1992?","The GA decided the FRY could not automatically continue the old Yugoslavia's seat. It was admitted as a new member in 2000."],
  ["membership","Which state has tried to leave the UN?","Indonesia announced its withdrawal in 1965 (protesting Malaysia's SC seat) and returned in 1966. The only state ever to try."],
  ["membership","Can Human Rights Council membership be suspended? Examples?","Yes, by a two-thirds GA vote: Libya (2011) and Russia (2022)."],
  ["membership","Suspension vs expulsion vs loss of GA vote vs credentials rejection: what is the difference?","Suspension (Art. 5) follows SC enforcement action and was never used. Expulsion (Art. 6) is for persistent violations and was never used. Loss of vote (Art. 19) is for arrears. A credentials rejection (South Africa 1974) bars a delegation without touching membership."],
  ["membership","Why is Taiwan not a UN member?","GA resolution 2758 (XXVI) of 1971 gave China's seat, including its permanent SC seat, to the PRC. Few states recognize Taiwan."],
  ["membership","Why is Kosovo not a UN member?","It declared independence from Serbia in 2008. Roughly 100+ states recognize it, but not Russia or China, who would block admission."],
  ["membership","What did the ICJ say about Kosovo in 2010?","Its advisory opinion found the declaration of independence did not violate international law."],
  ["membership","Western Sahara (SADR): why is it not a UN member?","It is on the UN list of Non-Self-Governing Territories and claimed by Morocco. The SADR is an African Union member. MINURSO was set up in 1991."],
  ["membership","What did SC resolution 2797 (October 2025) do?","It backed Morocco's autonomy plan as a basis for negotiations on Western Sahara. (Check news.)"],
  ["membership","Somaliland: why is it not a UN member?","Declared independence from Somalia in 1991 and functions as a state, but has little or no formal recognition. The AU defends Somalia's territorial integrity."],
  ["membership","Northern Cyprus: why is it not a UN member?","Recognized only by Türkiye. UN peacekeeping force UNFICYP since 1964."],
  ["membership","Which observer organisations attend the GA?","The EU, AU, ICRC, IOC and others. They can attend and speak but not vote."],
  ["membership","What can observers do and not do?","Attend and usually speak, but cannot vote. Observer states do not sign the Charter."],
  ["membership","List the entities that are not UN members and the reason for each.","Taiwan (China's seat went to the PRC, 1971). Kosovo (Russia and China would block). Western Sahara (a non-self-governing territory claimed by Morocco). Somaliland (little recognition, AU opposes). Northern Cyprus (only Türkiye recognizes it)."],
  ["membership","How did the ROC lose the China seat, what did A/RES/2758 say, and what about returning?","• Taiwan: how did the ROC lose the China seat: The ROC was a founding member with a permanent SC seat. After losing the civil war in 1949 it moved to Taiwan. It kept China's seat for two decades, backed by the US. By 1971 the PRC had wide recognition.\n• What did A/RES/2758 (25 October 1971) say: It recognized the PRC's representatives as \"the only lawful representatives of China\" and expelled \"the representatives of Chiang Kai-shek\".\n• What happened to Taiwan's attempts to return to the UN: From 1993 to 2008 (latterly as \"Taiwan\"), the GA's General Committee rejected them."],
  ["membership","Who recognizes Kosovo and who opposes it?","Around 100+ states recognize it (counts disputed, as some have withdrawn recognition). The US, UK and France support it. Russia and China, with Serbia, oppose it, so it cannot pass the SC."],
  ["membership","When did the ICJ advise on Kosovo, and what was its reasoning?","22 July 2010. The declaration \"did not violate international law\", because international law has no prohibition on declarations of independence."],
  ["membership","Somaliland: history, and why no broad recognition?","• Somaliland: what is its history: Former British Somaliland was briefly independent (26 June 1960), then united with Italian Somalia on 1 July 1960. It declared independence in 1991 after the Somali state collapsed, and has held elections and had its own currency and army since.\n• Why doesn't Somaliland have broad recognition: 1. Somalia's federal government claims it. 2. The AU defends colonial-era borders (uti possidetis) and fears a precedent for secession across Africa. 3. Admission needs SC approval."],
  ["membership","Western Sahara: from Spanish colony to the stalled MINURSO referendum?","• Western Sahara: what was it before 1975: A Spanish colony until 1975.\n• What did the ICJ say on Western Sahara in 1975: Advisory opinion of 16 October 1975: historical ties to Morocco and Mauritania, but no tie of territorial sovereignty, so self-determination applied.\n• Madrid Accords (November 1975): Transferred administration of Western Sahara to Morocco and Mauritania.\n• Polisario Front and the SADR: Polisario proclaimed the SADR in 1976 and fought both Morocco and Mauritania. Mauritania withdrew in 1979.\n• Why did the MINURSO referendum never happen: A UN-brokered ceasefire in 1991 created MINURSO to organize a referendum, but the parties disagreed on voter eligibility."],
  ["membership","Northern Cyprus: division, declaration, recognition and who keeps the peace?","• How was Cyprus divided: After a Greek-backed coup and Türkiye's military intervention in 1974.\n• When was the \"Turkish Republic of Northern Cyprus\" declared, and what did the SC say: 1983. The Security Council called the declaration legally invalid.\n• Who recognizes Northern Cyprus, and what keeps the peace there: Only Türkiye. UNFICYP has kept the buffer zone since 1964."],
  ["membership","What is the GA Credentials Committee?","A GA body that checks the credentials of governments' delegations. The UN accepts government credentials; it does not \"recognize\" states."],
  ["membership","The Holy See vs Palestine: how long has each been an observer?","The Holy See since 1964. Palestine since 2012, as an \"observer state\"."],
  ["membership","How many states can vote in the General Assembly?","193 members. Observer states and organisations cannot vote."],
  ["membership","Which applicants does the veto block?","Any applicant whose admission a P5 member opposes. The doc's example is Kosovo, which Russia and China would block."],
  ["membership","In 30 seconds: how does a state join the UN, and what does the UN's role on recognition mean?","It applies to the Secretary-General, the Security Council recommends (9 votes, no veto), and the GA admits by two-thirds. The UN admits members and accepts credentials; only states recognize states. That is why Taiwan, Kosovo and others stay outside."],
  ["system","Name the six principal organs (Article 7).","General Assembly, Security Council, Economic and Social Council, Trusteeship Council (inactive since 1994), International Court of Justice, Secretariat."],
  ["system","Where are the six principal organs based?","Five are in New York. The ICJ sits in The Hague."],
  ["system","UN funds and programmes vs specialized agencies vs related organizations: examples?","• UN funds and programmes: examples and key feature: UNDP, UNICEF, WFP, UNFPA, UNEP, UN-Habitat, UNHCR (an office). Created by the GA, run on voluntary funding, governed by executive boards.\n• UN specialized agencies: examples and key feature: WHO, ILO, FAO, UNESCO, IMF, World Bank, ICAO, IMO, ITU, WIPO, UNIDO, IFAD, UPU, WMO, UN Tourism. Autonomous organizations with their own treaties, members and budgets, linked to the UN through ECOSOC.\n• UN related organizations: examples and key feature: IAEA, WTO, OPCW, IOM, CTBTO. Separate, with special working agreements. The IAEA reports to the GA and SC."],
  ["system","Which Charter articles link specialized agencies to the UN?","Articles 57 and 63 (through ECOSOC)."],
  ["system","Funds and programmes vs specialized agencies vs related organizations?","Funds and programmes: created by the GA, voluntary funding, executive boards. Specialized agencies: autonomous, own treaties and budgets, linked via ECOSOC. Related organizations: separate, with special agreements."],
  ["system","What is UNHCR's status within the UN system?","An office, listed under funds and programmes."],
  ["system","Main UN offices and what each hosts?","New York (HQ). Geneva (UNOG: human rights). Vienna (UNOV: UNODC, IAEA, CTBTO). Nairobi (UNON: UNEP, UN-Habitat)."],
  ["system","Which main UN office is in the Global South?","Nairobi (UNON), the only HQ there."],
  ["system","The six official languages of the UN?","Arabic, Chinese, English, French, Russian, Spanish."],
  ["system","Working languages of the Secretariat?","English and French."],
  ["system","Assessed vs voluntary UN funding: what is each and what does it pay for?","• UN assessed contributions: what are they and how are they set: Compulsory, set by the GA's scale of assessments, based mostly on gross national income.\n• What do voluntary contributions fund: Most humanitarian and development work (UNICEF, WFP, UNHCR, UNDP). They are the majority of total UN system money.\n• Assessed vs voluntary funding: which pays for what: Assessed contributions are compulsory and fund the regular budget. Voluntary contributions fund most humanitarian and development work and are the majority of total system money."],
  ["system","Who handles the budget in the General Assembly?","The Fifth Committee, advised by the ACABQ."],
  ["system","What is the UN liquidity crisis?","Late and unpaid contributions, especially big cuts and arrears from the United States since 2025, which forced hiring freezes and cuts."],
  ["system","What is the 2030 Agenda?","The 17 SDGs (A/RES/70/1, 2015)."],
  ["system","When is the next SDG Summit at heads-of-state level?","2027."],
  ["system","What is \"A New Agenda for Peace\"?","A 2023 policy brief: prevention, disarmament, regional approaches."],
  ["system","Major crises on the UN agenda?","Gaza and the West Bank, Sudan, Ukraine, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Haiti, Myanmar, the Sahel, Yemen, Afghanistan."],
  ["system","Which crisis is the world's largest displacement crisis?","Sudan."],
  ["system","UN successes to name?","• UN successes worth naming: No war between great powers since 1945. Decolonization (over 80 former colonies became members). The UDHR and human rights treaties. Peacekeeping (Nobel Prize 1988). Eradicating smallpox (WHO, 1980). The Montreal Protocol on the ozone layer. The NPT. Feeding and sheltering millions (WFP, UNHCR, UNICEF).\n• UN criticisms worth naming: Veto paralysis (Syria, Ukraine, Gaza). Failures in Rwanda (1994) and Srebrenica (1995). A Security Council that reflects 1945, not today. Non-binding resolutions. Dependence on a few big funders. Bureaucracy and duplication. Peacekeeping abuses (Haiti cholera, sexual exploitation).\n• How many former colonies became UN members through decolonization: Over 80.\n• What disease was eradicated through the UN, and when: Smallpox (WHO, 1980).\n• Which treaty protects the ozone layer: The Montreal Protocol.\n• When did UN peacekeeping win the Nobel Peace Prize: 1988."],
  ["system","UN failures to name (veto paralysis, classic failures, peacekeeping abuses)?","• Which crises show veto paralysis: Syria, Ukraine, Gaza.\n• Which failures are the classic examples of UN failure: Rwanda (1994) and Srebrenica (1995).\n• Which peacekeeping abuses are criticised: Haiti cholera and sexual exploitation."],
  ["system","How should you structure an answer on UN successes and criticisms?","Balance both sides, then end with what reform is being discussed."],
  ["system","In 30 seconds: is the UN still relevant?","It has kept great powers from war since 1945, driven decolonization and human rights law, and feeds and shelters millions. But the veto paralyses the Council, the Council reflects 1945, funding is shaky, and past failures (Rwanda, Srebrenica) hurt. Reform, such as Council enlargement, is the live debate."],
  ["system","How many principal organs are active today?","Five. The Trusteeship Council has been inactive since 1994."],
  ["system","What do funds and programmes and specialized agencies have in common, and how do they differ?","Both are part of the wider UN system. Funds and programmes are created by the GA and run on voluntary funding. Specialized agencies are autonomous, with their own treaties and budgets."],
  ["system","What does the UN office in Vienna (UNOV) host?","UNODC, IAEA, CTBTO."],
  ["system","What kind of UN body is UNEP, and where is it based?","A fund or programme, based in Nairobi."],
  ["system","What advises the Fifth Committee on budget matters?","The ACABQ."],
  ["ga","What is the General Assembly, and which Charter articles cover it?","The UN's main deliberative, policymaking and representative organ (Chapter IV, Articles 9–22). All 193 members sit in it, one state one vote."],
  ["ga","GA Articles 10, 12, 13 and 17: what does each do?","• GA: Article 10: The GA can discuss any question within the Charter and make recommendations, which are not legally binding.\n• GA: Article 12: The GA should not recommend on a dispute the Security Council is actively handling.\n• GA: Article 17: The GA approves the budget and the scale of assessments. This is binding.\n• GA: Article 13: The GA initiates studies and codification of international law, mostly through the International Law Commission."],
  ["ga","What does the GA elect, and how does it appoint the Secretary-General and admit members?","• Whose members does the GA elect: The 10 non-permanent SC members, the 54 ECOSOC members and the 47 HRC members. With the SC, it elects ICJ judges.\n• How does the GA appoint the Secretary-General and admit new members: On the Security Council's recommendation, in both cases."],
  ["ga","List the GA's main powers.","Discuss anything in the Charter (Art. 10) and recommend. Budget and scale of assessments (Art. 17, binding). Elect SC non-permanent, ECOSOC and HRC members, and ICJ judges (with the SC). Appoint the SG and admit members on the SC's recommendation. Codify international law (Art. 13). Consider reports from other organs."],
  ["ga","How does GA voting work (Article 18)?","\"Important questions\" need two-thirds of members present and voting. Other questions need a simple majority."],
  ["ga","Which GA questions count as \"important\"?","Peace and security recommendations, elections, admission, budget."],
  ["ga","How are most GA resolutions adopted in practice?","Without a vote, by consensus."],
  ["ga","When does the GA regular session open?","Each September. The high-level general debate follows a week later."],
  ["ga","When did the 81st GA session open?","8 September 2026."],
  ["ga","Who is President of the GA for the 81st session?","Khalilur Rahman of Bangladesh, elected 2 June 2026."],
  ["ga","GA General Committee and Credentials Committee: who sits and what do they do?","• GA General Committee: who sits and what does it do: The President, 21 vice-presidents and the six Main Committee chairs. It organizes the agenda.\n• GA Credentials Committee: size and role: 9 members. It checks delegations' credentials."],
  ["ga","Which kinds of special sessions can the GA hold?","Special sessions and emergency special sessions."],
  ["ga","First Committee (DISEC)?","Disarmament and international security. Works with the UN Disarmament Commission and the Conference on Disarmament in Geneva. On the NMUN 2027 list."],
  ["ga","Second Committee (ECOFIN)?","Economic and financial: growth, development finance, trade, debt, poverty, sustainable development, climate. On the NMUN 2027 list."],
  ["ga","Third Committee (SOCHUM)?","Social, humanitarian and cultural: human rights, refugees, women, children, crime and drugs. Considers the HRC's annual report; over half its resolutions are on human rights. On the NMUN 2027 list."],
  ["ga","Fourth Committee (SPECPOL)?","Special political and decolonization: Non-Self-Governing Territories, peacekeeping review, UNRWA, outer space, atomic radiation. Formed by merging the original decolonization committee with the Special Political Committee (1993)."],
  ["ga","Fifth Committee?","Administrative and budgetary. Usually decides by consensus."],
  ["ga","Sixth Committee?","Legal. Receives the International Law Commission's report and drafts conventions."],
  ["ga","Name the six GA Main Committees and their subjects.","1st DISEC (disarmament and security), 2nd ECOFIN (economic and financial), 3rd SOCHUM (social, humanitarian, cultural), 4th SPECPOL (special political and decolonization), 5th administrative and budgetary, 6th legal."],
  ["ga","Examples of GA subsidiary bodies (committees, commissions, boards, programmes)?","• Examples of GA subsidiary bodies: Human Rights Council, Peacebuilding Commission (with the SC), International Law Commission, UN Disarmament Commission, UNCITRAL, Special Committee on Decolonization (C-24), High-Level Political Forum (with ECOSOC), and the executive boards of funds and programmes.\n• GA subsidiary committees (examples): Six Main Committees; General Committee; Credentials Committee; Committee on Contributions; ACABQ (budget advice); Special Committee on Decolonization (C-24).\n• GA subsidiary commissions (examples): International Law Commission (1947, 34 experts); UNCITRAL; UN Disarmament Commission; International Civil Service Commission; UN Conciliation Commission for Palestine (Res. 194, 1948); Peacebuilding Commission (with the SC).\n• Which GA council replaced a commission, and when: The Human Rights Council (2006) replaced ECOSOC's Commission on Human Rights.\n• GA subsidiary boards (examples): Executive Boards of UNICEF, UNDP/UNFPA/UNOPS, WFP (shared with FAO), UN Women; Board of Auditors; Trade and Development Board; UN Joint Staff Pension Board; Advisory Board on Disarmament Matters.\n• GA working groups and others (examples): Open-ended working groups (e.g. on ICT security), ad hoc committees (e.g. the one that negotiated the Cybercrime Convention), the High-Level Political Forum (with ECOSOC).\n• GA funds and programmes, with creation dates: UNICEF (1946), UNHCR (1950), UNDP (1965), UNEP (1972), UNFPA, WFP, UN-Habitat, UNRWA (1949), UN Women (2010)."],
  ["ga","Uniting for Peace: what is it, what triggers it, when first used?","• What is Uniting for Peace: A/RES/377 (V), 3 November 1950, during the Korean War. If the SC fails to act because the P5 are not unanimous, the GA can meet in emergency special session and recommend collective measures.\n• What can trigger a Uniting for Peace emergency special session: A procedural SC vote (any nine members, no veto) or a majority of GA members.\n• When was Uniting for Peace first used: The Suez Crisis (1956), which led to UNEF I."],
  ["ga","When are GA decisions binding (opinio juris, binding decisions)?","• Can GA resolutions become binding: Not by themselves. But they can be evidence of customary international law when they reflect state practice and opinio juris. The UDHR is the classic example. GA decisions on internal matters (budget, elections, procedure) are binding.\n• What is opinio juris: The belief that something is legally required.\n• Which GA decisions are binding: Those on internal matters: the budget, elections and its own procedure."],
  ["ga","General Assembly vs Security Council: compare members, outcome, voting, scope, meetings, strength?","• Members: General Assembly vs Security Council: GA: all 193. SC: 15.\n• Outcome: General Assembly vs Security Council: GA: recommendations (binding only on internal matters). SC: binding decisions under Chapter VII.\n• Voting: General Assembly vs Security Council: GA: one state one vote, two-thirds for important questions. SC: 9 votes, no P5 veto on substance.\n• Scope: General Assembly vs Security Council: GA: anything within the Charter. SC: peace and security.\n• Meetings: General Assembly vs Security Council: GA: regular session September to December, resumed through the year. SC: continuously; it must be able to meet at any time.\n• Strength: General Assembly vs Security Council: GA: universal legitimacy, sets norms, controls the budget. SC: power to act."],
  ["ga","In 30 seconds: what is the General Assembly and what can it do?","The UN's universal body: 193 members, one vote each. It debates anything in the Charter and makes non-binding recommendations, but it controls the budget, elects members of other organs, and can bypass a Security Council veto through Uniting for Peace."],
  ["sc","What is the Security Council's role, and which Charter articles cover it?","Primary responsibility for international peace and security (Art. 24). It covers Chapters V–VIII of the Charter (Arts. 23–54)."],
  ["sc","Why is the Security Council unique among UN organs?","It is the only organ whose decisions bind all members (Art. 25)."],
  ["sc","How many members does the Security Council have, and who are the P5?","15 members. Five permanent (P5): China, France, Russia, the United Kingdom, the United States."],
  ["sc","How are the E10 chosen, split by region, and which seat alternates?","• How are the ten elected Security Council members (E10) chosen: By the GA by two-thirds, for two-year terms. Five each year, with no immediate re-election.\n• How are the ten elected Security Council seats split by region: Africa 3, Asia-Pacific 2, Latin America and Caribbean 2, Western Europe and Others 2, Eastern Europe 1.\n• Which Security Council seat alternates between regions: One seat alternates between Asia-Pacific and Africa as an Arab seat."],
  ["sc","Elected Security Council members 2026–2027?","Bahrain, Colombia, Democratic Republic of the Congo, Latvia, Liberia."],
  ["sc","Who sits on the Security Council in 2026, in full?","P5 plus Denmark, Greece, Pakistan, Panama, Somalia (terms end 2026) and Bahrain, Colombia, DR Congo, Latvia, Liberia (terms end 2027)."],
  ["sc","Security Council presidency: how it rotates and what the president can do?","• How does the Security Council presidency rotate: Monthly, in English alphabetical order.\n• What can the Security Council president do: Chair meetings, call meetings, and issue presidential statements (adopted by consensus) and press statements."],
  ["sc","Veto record: who uses it most, since 2011, early Cold War, since 1970, Russia since 2022?","• Who uses the Security Council veto most: The USSR/Russia (well over 100 times), then the United States (about 90, many on Israel-Palestine).\n• Which vetoes have been used since 2011: Russia and China have vetoed repeatedly on Syria. Russia on Ukraine. The US on Gaza ceasefire drafts.\n• Who cast most vetoes in the early Cold War, and why: The USSR, partly to block new members.\n• Which state has vetoed most since 1970: The United States, many times on Israel–Palestine, including several Gaza ceasefire drafts since October 2023.\n• What has Russia vetoed since 2022: Resolutions on Ukraine, including on its own aggression (February 2022). Russia, often with China, has also vetoed on Syria since 2011."],
  ["sc","What does Article 27(3) say about parties to a dispute?","A member that is a party to a dispute should abstain on Chapter VI decisions about it, but this is rarely enforced."],
  ["sc","Security Council subsidiary bodies: sanctions committees, 1373 Committee, 1540 Committee, Military Staff Committee?","• Security Council sanctions committees: how many regimes: Around 14 regimes.\n• Which committee did S/RES/1373 (2001) create: The Counter-Terrorism Committee.\n• What is the 1540 Committee: The Security Council committee on weapons of mass destruction and non-state actors.\n• What is the Military Staff Committee: A body under Article 47. Largely dormant.\n• List the Security Council's subsidiary bodies.: Sanctions committees (around 14 regimes), the Counter-Terrorism Committee (S/RES/1373, 2001), the 1540 Committee on WMD and non-state actors, the Military Staff Committee (Art. 47, largely dormant), peacekeeping missions, and the Peacebuilding Commission (shared with the GA).\n• Which Security Council bodies deal with sanctions: The sanctions committees."],
  ["sc","Which body is shared between the Security Council and the General Assembly?","The Peacebuilding Commission."],
  ["sc","S/RES 1325, 2250 and 2719: what does each cover?","• S/RES/1325 (2000): Women, Peace and Security.\n• S/RES/2250 (2015): Youth, Peace and Security.\n• S/RES/2719 (2023): A framework for financing AU-led peace support operations from UN assessed contributions."],
  ["sc","Security Council meeting formats, who can speak, president's powers, presidential statements?","• What formats do Security Council meetings take: Public meetings (briefings and open debates), private meetings, and closed consultations where most negotiation happens.\n• Who can speak in public Security Council meetings: Non-members, under Rule 37.\n• What are the Security Council president's powers for the month: Sets the programme of work, calls meetings (including at a member's or the Secretary-General's request), chairs, negotiates and reads presidential statements, issues press statements, and briefs non-members on the month's work.\n• How are Security Council presidential statements published: As S/PRST/…, adopted by consensus."],
  ["sc","Security Council outcomes, from strongest to weakest?","Resolutions (S/RES/…), presidential statements, press statements, letters."],
  ["sc","Which non-members may take part in Security Council debates?","Directly affected non-members may take part without a vote (Article 31). Parties to a dispute are invited (Article 32)."],
  ["sc","Charter Articles 24, 25, 27, 31 and 32: what do they say?","• What is Article 24 of the Charter: The Security Council has primary responsibility for peace and security.\n• What is Article 25 of the Charter: All members must carry out Security Council decisions.\n• What is Article 27 of the Charter: Security Council voting.\n• What are Articles 31 and 32 of the Charter: 31: directly affected non-members may take part without a vote. 32: parties to a dispute are invited."],
  ["sc","Security Council substantive vote: how many needed, and does an abstention block?","• How many votes does the Security Council need on substantive matters: Nine of 15, with no veto from any P5 member.\n• Does an abstention block a Security Council resolution: No. In practice an abstention is not a veto."],
  ["sc","How many Security Council seats does Eastern Europe hold?","1."],
  ["sc","What is the E10?","The ten elected non-permanent Security Council members."],
  ["sc","Where does most Security Council negotiation happen?","In closed consultations."],
  ["sc","Which two Security Council resolutions cover women and youth?","S/RES/1325 (women) and S/RES/2250 (youth)."],
  ["sc","How many elected seats does Africa hold on the Security Council?","3."],
  ["sc","In 30 seconds: how does the Security Council work?","15 members: five permanent with a veto and ten elected for two years. Nine votes are needed, with no P5 veto on substance. It alone can bind all members, through Chapter VII, but one P5 \"no\" can paralyse it, as with Syria, Ukraine and Gaza."],
  ["ecosoc","What is ECOSOC, and which Charter articles cover it?","The principal body for coordination, policy review and dialogue on economic, social and environmental issues, and for follow-up to the SDGs and major UN conferences (Chapter X, Arts. 61–72)."],
  ["ecosoc","ECOSOC membership, growth to 54, and how it decides?","• ECOSOC membership: how many members, elected how, for how long: 54 members, elected by the GA for three-year terms, 18 each year. Immediate re-election is allowed.\n• How did ECOSOC grow to 54 members: Enlarged from 18 to 27 (1965) and to 54 (1973) by Charter amendment.\n• How does ECOSOC decide, and how long does its President serve: By simple majority. The President serves one year."],
  ["ecosoc","ECOSOC Article 62, what it prepares for the GA, coordination of agencies, other segments?","• ECOSOC: Article 62: Studies, reports and recommendations on economic, social, cultural, educational and health matters.\n• What does ECOSOC prepare for the GA: Draft conventions. It also calls international conferences.\n• How does ECOSOC coordinate specialized agencies: Through agreements with them (Arts. 57, 63).\n• Other ECOSOC segments and forums: The Humanitarian Affairs Segment, the Youth Forum, the Financing for Development Forum, and the Operational Activities Segment on UN development work."],
  ["ecosoc","In 30 seconds: what does ECOSOC do, and can it bind states?","It coordinates and reviews economic, social and environmental work across the UN system, follows up the SDGs and major conferences, studies and recommends, and prepares draft conventions for the GA. Its decisions are recommendations, not binding."],
  ["ecosoc","ECOSOC functional commissions: name them (CSW, CPD, CCPCJ, CND…)?","• Name ECOSOC's functional commissions.: CSW, CPD, CCPCJ, CND, Statistical Commission, Commission for Social Development, Commission on Science and Technology for Development, UN Forum on Forests (eight).\n• CSW: Commission on the Status of Women. 45 members.\n• CPD: Commission on Population and Development. 47 members.\n• CCPCJ: Commission on Crime Prevention and Criminal Justice. 40 members, Vienna.\n• CND: Commission on Narcotic Drugs. 53 members, Vienna.\n• How many functional commissions does ECOSOC have: Eight."],
  ["ecosoc","ECOSOC five regional commissions and their seats?","• Name ECOSOC's five regional commissions and their seats.: ECA (Addis Ababa), ECE (Geneva), ECLAC (Santiago), ESCAP (Bangkok), ESCWA (Beirut).\n• ECA: Economic Commission for Africa, Addis Ababa.\n• ECE: Economic Commission for Europe, Geneva.\n• ECLAC: Economic Commission for Latin America and the Caribbean, Santiago.\n• ESCAP: Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the Pacific, Bangkok.\n• ESCWA: Economic and Social Commission for Western Asia, Beirut."],
  ["ecosoc","Other ECOSOC bodies: Permanent Forum, CDP, tax committee, NGO committee, CPC, public administration?","• Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues: An ECOSOC expert body, created 2000.\n• Committee for Development Policy: An ECOSOC expert body that reviews the list of Least Developed Countries (decides LDC status).\n• Committee of Experts on International Cooperation in Tax Matters: An ECOSOC expert body (the Tax Committee).\n• ECOSOC Committee on NGOs: A standing committee of 19 members. It recommends consultative status.\n• ECOSOC Committee for Programme and Coordination: An ECOSOC standing committee.\n• ECOSOC Committee of Experts on Public Administration: An ECOSOC expert body."],
  ["ecosoc","Commission vs committee vs council in UN usage?","A commission is a standing subsidiary body that studies and recommends in one field (CSW). A committee deals with assigned agenda items (GA Main Committees) or monitors a treaty (treaty bodies). A council is a higher-level policy or decision body (SC, HRC, ECOSOC)."],
  ["ecosoc","How do specialized agencies relate to ECOSOC, and what is special about the IMF and World Bank?","• How does each specialized agency relate to ECOSOC: Under Articles 57 and 63, each agency signs a relationship agreement with ECOSOC.\n• Which specialized agencies does ECOSOC coordinate: ILO, FAO, UNESCO, WHO, the World Bank Group, IMF, ICAO, IMO, ITU, UPU, WMO, WIPO, IFAD, UNIDO and UN Tourism.\n• What is special about the IMF and World Bank as specialized agencies: They are formally specialized agencies but run largely independently, with weighted voting."],
  ["ecosoc","Which ECOSOC functional commissions meet in Vienna?","CND and CCPCJ."],
  ["ecosoc","Which ECOSOC body recommends NGO consultative status?","The Committee on NGOs (19 members)."],
  ["icj","What is the ICJ, where does it sit, and what did it replace?","The UN's principal judicial organ (Art. 92), sitting in the Peace Palace in The Hague. It replaced the League's Permanent Court of International Justice (PCIJ) in 1946."],
  ["icj","How is the ICJ Statute linked to the UN, and who is a party to it?","The Statute is annexed to the Charter. Every UN member is automatically a party to it (Art. 93)."],
  ["icj","ICJ judges: number, terms, election, bench rules, judge ad hoc?","• ICJ judges: how many, what terms, how often elected: 15 judges, nine-year terms, a third elected every three years (five judges, in November). Re-election is allowed.\n• How are ICJ judges elected: By an absolute majority in both the GA and the SC, voting separately. No veto applies.\n• ICJ bench composition rules: No two judges of the same nationality. The bench should represent the main legal systems of the world.\n• What is a judge ad hoc: A state in a case with no judge of its nationality may appoint one."],
  ["icj","Who are the ICJ President and Vice-President?","• Who is the ICJ President: Iwasawa Yuji (Japan), from March 2025, after Nawaf Salam (Lebanon) resigned to become Prime Minister of Lebanon.\n• Who is the ICJ Vice-President: Julia Sebutinde (Uganda)."],
  ["icj","ICJ contentious cases: who can bring them, and are judgments binding?","Disputes between states only. Requires consent. Judgments are final and binding (Art. 94); if a party ignores one, the other may go to the SC."],
  ["icj","ICJ advisory opinions: who can ask, and are they binding?","The GA or SC, or other organs and agencies authorized by the GA (such as WHO) within their field (Art. 96). Not binding, but highly authoritative."],
  ["icj","Contentious cases vs advisory opinions?","Contentious: disputes between states, needs consent, binding. Advisory: legal questions from the GA, SC or authorized agencies, not binding but highly authoritative."],
  ["icj","What happened with the United States and the ICJ optional clause?","The US withdrew its optional clause acceptance in 1986 after Nicaragua v. United States. It is still a party to the Statute, as are all UN members."],
  ["icj","ICJ vs ICC: who they try, legal basis, UN link, subject matter?","• ICJ vs ICC: who do they try: ICJ: states. ICC: individuals.\n• ICJ vs ICC: legal basis: ICJ: UN Charter and ICJ Statute (1945). ICC: Rome Statute (1998, in force 2002).\n• ICJ vs ICC: relationship to the UN: ICJ: a principal UN organ. ICC: independent; the SC can refer situations and defer cases.\n• ICJ vs ICC: subject matter: ICJ: any international legal dispute. ICC: genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes, aggression.\n• ICJ vs ICC in one answer.: Both sit in The Hague. The ICJ settles disputes between states and is a UN organ; the ICC tries individuals for the worst crimes and is independent of the UN."],
  ["icj","ICJ landmark cases and opinions to know?","• Nicaragua v. United States (1986): US support for the Contras and mining of harbours violated the ban on force and non-intervention. The US rejected the ruling.\n• The Wall advisory opinion (2004): Israel's separation wall in the occupied West Bank is contrary to international law.\n• Kosovo advisory opinion (2010): The declaration of independence was not unlawful.\n• The Gambia v. Myanmar (2019–): The Rohingya genocide case. Any party to the Genocide Convention can sue (erga omnes partes).\n• Which ICJ landmark cases and opinions should you know: Nicaragua v. US (1986), The Wall (advisory, 2004), Kosovo (advisory, 2010), The Gambia v. Myanmar (2019–)."],
  ["icj","In 30 seconds: what is the ICJ and what can it do?","The UN's principal judicial organ in The Hague. 15 judges elected by the GA and SC. It settles disputes between consenting states with binding judgments and gives non-binding advisory opinions to UN organs. It cannot try individuals; that is the ICC."],
  ["secretariat","What does the Secretariat do, and which Charter articles cover it?","Chapter XV, Arts. 97–101. It carries out the day-to-day work mandated by the other organs: preparing reports, servicing meetings, running peace operations, mediating and gathering data."],
  ["secretariat","What is the status of Secretariat staff?","International civil servants who must not take instructions from any government (Art. 100)."],
  ["secretariat","Articles 97 and 99: what do they say and when did Guterres invoke 99?","• Article 97: The Secretary-General is the \"chief administrative officer\" of the UN.\n• Article 99: The Secretary-General may bring to the SC's attention any matter that may threaten peace and security.\n• When did Guterres formally invoke Article 99: Over Gaza, in December 2023, the first explicit use in decades."],
  ["secretariat","Secretary-General: good offices, annual report, and which body does he chair?","• What are the Secretary-General's \"good offices\": Quiet diplomacy and mediation.\n• What annual report does the Secretary-General present: The Report on the Work of the Organization, to the GA.\n• Which body does the Secretary-General chair: The UN System Chief Executives Board for Coordination (CEB)."],
  ["secretariat","How is the Secretary-General selected (nomination, Council stage, final step)?","• How is the Secretary-General selected: nomination: Candidates are nominated by member states. Since 2016 they publish vision statements and face public dialogues in the GA (A/RES/69/321).\n• How is the Secretary-General selected: the Security Council stage: Secret straw polls (\"encourage\", \"discourage\" or \"no opinion\"), with P5 votes later colour-coded. Then a resolution recommending one candidate: 9 votes and no P5 veto.\n• How is the Secretary-General selected: the final step: The GA appoints by resolution."],
  ["secretariat","Secretary-General term, has there been a woman, which region is next?","• Secretary-General term: Five years, renewable. No one has served more than two terms.\n• Has there been a woman Secretary-General: No. There is strong pressure for one in 2026.\n• Which region is expected to supply the next Secretary-General: Latin America and the Caribbean, by informal regional rotation."],
  ["secretariat","Race to succeed Guterres: state of play and how to answer in the interview?","• State of the race to succeed Guterres (late October 2026): The SC's straw polls were deadlocked. Reported leading names include Rebeca Grynspan (Costa Rica, head of UN Trade and Development) and Rafael Grossi (Argentina, IAEA Director-General). The new SG takes office on 1 January 2027.\n• Who will succeed Guterres? How would you answer in the interview: Say it is still open: the SC's straw polls are deadlocked, and reported leading names are Rebeca Grynspan and Rafael Grossi. The new SG takes office on 1 January 2027, and Latin America and the Caribbean is the expected region."],
  ["secretariat","Trygve Lie: what to remember?","Resigned after Soviet hostility over his support for the UN in Korea."],
  ["secretariat","Dag Hammarskjöld: what to remember?","Created UNEF I peacekeeping (1956). Died in a plane crash near Ndola, Northern Rhodesia (now Zambia), on a Congo peace mission. Posthumous Nobel."],
  ["secretariat","U Thant: what to remember?","First non-European SG. Mediated in the Cuban Missile Crisis."],
  ["secretariat","Kurt Waldheim: what to remember?","Third term blocked by China. Later controversy over his wartime past; became Austria's president."],
  ["secretariat","Javier Pérez de Cuéllar: what to remember?","Only Latin American SG so far. Iran–Iraq ceasefire (1988)."],
  ["secretariat","Boutros Boutros-Ghali: what to remember?","First African and first Arab SG. An Agenda for Peace (1992). Department of Peacekeeping Operations created (1992). Second term vetoed by the US. In office during Rwanda and Srebrenica."],
  ["secretariat","Kofi Annan: what to remember?","Millennium Development Goals. Nobel Peace Prize 2001 (shared with the UN). R2P debate. Human Rights Council created. Only black SG so far."],
  ["secretariat","Ban Ki-moon: what to remember?","SDGs and the Paris Agreement. UN Women created (2010)."],
  ["secretariat","António Guterres: what to remember?","Former Prime Minister of Portugal and UN High Commissioner for Refugees. Pact for the Future. UN80 reforms."],
  ["secretariat","Name the nine Secretaries-General in order.","Lie (Norway), Hammarskjöld (Sweden), U Thant (Burma), Waldheim (Austria), Pérez de Cuéllar (Peru), Boutros-Ghali (Egypt), Annan (Ghana), Ban Ki-moon (South Korea), Guterres (Portugal)."],
  ["secretariat","Who is the Deputy Secretary-General?","Amina J. Mohammed (Nigeria), since 2017."],
  ["secretariat","Trusteeship Council: what it did, when and why it stopped, who sits?","• Trusteeship Council: what did it do: Supervised 11 Trust Territories (former League mandates and territories taken from the Axis) toward self-government.\n• Trusteeship Council: when did it stop, and why: The last Trust Territory, Palau, became independent in 1994. The Council suspended operations on 1 November 1994 and now meets only if needed.\n• Who sits on the Trusteeship Council, and how could it be abolished: Its members are the P5. Abolishing it would need a Charter amendment."],
  ["secretariat","Secretaries-General: who rose from staff, had a second term vetoed, third term blocked, died in office?","• Which Secretary-General rose from the UN staff: Kofi Annan, the only one.\n• Which Secretary-General had a second term vetoed, and by whom: Boutros Boutros-Ghali, by the US in 1996 (the vote being 14–1).\n• Which Secretary-General had a third term blocked by China: Kurt Waldheim.\n• Which Secretary-General died in office, and where: Dag Hammarskjöld, in a plane crash near Ndola, Northern Rhodesia (now Zambia), 18 September 1961."],
  ["secretariat","What are SRSGs?","Special Representatives and Special Envoys of the Secretary-General: private mediation and envoys in conflicts (good offices)."],
  ["secretariat","Secretary-General's agenda-setting reports, convening power, administration, and Fifth Committee budget role?","• The Secretary-General's agenda-setting reports: An Agenda for Peace, In Larger Freedom, Our Common Agenda (2021), A New Agenda for Peace (2023).\n• How does the Secretary-General convene: Summits and coordination through the Chief Executives Board.\n• What does the Secretary-General do on administration: Appoints staff, proposes the budget (approved by the GA), and runs peace operations day to day.\n• Secretariat vs Fifth Committee on the budget: The Secretariat (led by the SG) prepares the budget proposal and spends the money. The Fifth Committee (all member states, advised by the ACABQ) reviews, negotiates and approves it, and the GA plenary adopts it. The Secretariat proposes and implements; member states decide."],
  ["secretariat","In 30 seconds: what is the Secretariat and what can the Secretary-General do beyond the Charter text?","The UN's international civil service, led by the SG as chief administrative officer. The SG can raise threats to peace with the SC (Art. 99), mediate through good offices, send envoys, set the agenda with major reports, and convene summits."],
  ["intlaw","What is international law, and what is the difference between public and private international law?","The body of rules that binds states and international organizations in their relations with each other. Public international law governs states; private international law deals with cross-border disputes between private parties (which country's law applies)."],
  ["intlaw","What are the sources of international law (Article 38(1) of the ICJ Statute)?","1. Treaties (conventions). 2. Customary international law. 3. General principles of law recognized by nations. 4. Judicial decisions and the writings of leading scholars, as \"subsidiary means\"."],
  ["intlaw","Treaties as a source of law: who do they bind?","Only states that have consented."],
  ["intlaw","Customary international law: what is it and who does it bind?","General state practice accepted as law (opinio juris). It binds all states, except a persistent objector that objected clearly and consistently while the rule was forming."],
  ["intlaw","General principles of law: examples and function?","Good faith, estoppel, pacta sunt servanda (treaties must be kept). Used to fill gaps."],
  ["intlaw","Judicial decisions and scholars as a source: what status?","\"Subsidiary means\" for identifying the law."],
  ["intlaw","Are GA resolutions a source of international law?","Not on the Article 38 list, but they can be evidence of custom."],
  ["intlaw","What is jus cogens?","Peremptory norms: rules from which no derogation is permitted. A treaty conflicting with one is void (VCLT Art. 53). The persistent objector rule does not apply to them."],
  ["intlaw","Jus cogens vs the R2P list: what is the difference?","R2P covers four crimes (genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing, crimes against humanity). Jus cogens is a broader category of norms. Do not mix them up."],
  ["intlaw","What is the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties (VCLT)?","The \"treaty on treaties\": adopted 1969, in force 1980."],
  ["intlaw","Where must treaties be registered?","With the UN Secretariat (Charter Art. 102)."],
  ["intlaw","Treaty vs convention vs covenant vs agreement?","All legally the same and binding for parties. \"Convention\" usually means a multilateral treaty open to all (UNFCCC); \"covenant\" is used for solemn rights treaties (ICCPR)."],
  ["intlaw","What is a protocol?","An add-on to a treaty (Kyoto Protocol to the UNFCCC). Binding for its parties; often optional."],
  ["intlaw","Are declarations binding?","Usually not. A political commitment (UDHR, Beijing Declaration)."],
  ["intlaw","When is a resolution binding, and what is soft law?","• When is a resolution binding: Only SC decisions under Chapter VII and GA internal decisions.\n• What is soft law: Non-binding guidelines, codes and frameworks that shape behaviour (Sendai Framework, Montreux Document)."],
  ["intlaw","Hard law vs soft law examples: treaty, protocol, declaration, resolution, soft law?","Treaty: binding for parties. Protocol: binding for its parties, often optional. Declaration: usually not binding. Resolution: binding only for SC Chapter VII decisions and GA internal decisions. Soft law: not binding."],
  ["intlaw","What is Westphalian sovereignty?","From the Peace of Westphalia (1648): each state has supreme authority over its territory and is equal in law; no interference in internal affairs. Reflected in Charter Arts. 2(1) and 2(7)."],
  ["intlaw","Montevideo criteria for statehood and declaratory vs constitutive theory?","• Montevideo Convention (1933), Article 1: the four criteria for statehood: (a) A permanent population, (b) a defined territory, (c) a government, (d) the capacity to enter into relations with other states. Recognition is not one of the four.\n• Declaratory vs constitutive theory of statehood: Declaratory: a state exists once it meets the criteria. Constitutive: a state exists only once others recognize it. Practice mixes both."],
  ["intlaw","Self-determination: which texts?","Charter Art. 1(2) and GA resolution 1514 (XV), 1960, the Decolonization Declaration."],
  ["intlaw","What is uti possidetis juris?","New states inherit colonial borders. A pillar of African Union policy."],
  ["intlaw","Which treaty governs diplomatic relations?","The Vienna Convention on Diplomatic Relations (1961)."],
  ["intlaw","Internal sovereignty, state practice and opinio juris, persistent objector?","• Internal sovereignty: definition and features: A state's authority and control over its own domestic affairs without interference from external actors. It involves making and enforcing laws, maintaining order and governing its population within its borders.\n• Customary law: what must state practice be, and what is opinio juris: State practice must be widespread, representative and consistent. Opinio juris is the belief it is legally required, not just habit or courtesy.\n• Persistent objector: when does it work: A state that objected clearly and consistently while the rule was forming is not bound. Rarely successful, and never against jus cogens."],
  ["intlaw","Define recognition, legitimacy, secession, occupation, annexation, colonization, entry into force, foreign policy?","• Recognition: A state's acknowledgement of another state or government; implies readiness for diplomatic relations. Only states grant it.\n• Legitimacy: Acceptance of a government's authority by its people and by other states.\n• Secession: A part of a state breaking away to form a new state, or formally withdrawing from a body.\n• Occupation: A foreign army's effective control of territory without sovereignty. Governed by the Hague Regulations (1907) and Geneva Convention IV.\n• Annexation: Claiming sovereignty over territory taken by force. Unlawful and not to be recognized.\n• Colonization: Settling among and establishing control over an area's indigenous people.\n• Entry into force: When a legal instrument starts to have legal effect.\n• Foreign policy: How a state deals with others, using diplomatic, political, economic, military and cultural tools."],
  ["intlaw","In 30 seconds: what are the sources of international law?","Treaties (bind consenting states), custom (state practice plus opinio juris, binds all but persistent objectors), general principles (good faith, estoppel), and judicial decisions and scholars as subsidiary means. GA resolutions are not sources but can be evidence of custom."],
  ["hr","UDHR: when was it adopted, and under which resolution?","10 December 1948, A/RES/217 A (III)."],
  ["hr","Is the UDHR binding?","No (a declaration), but much of it is now customary law."],
  ["hr","UDHR: structure, drafting and Human Rights Day?","Preamble and 30 articles; drafting committee chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt; 10 December is Human Rights Day."],
  ["hr","ICCPR: dates and rights covered?","• ICCPR: adopted and in force when: Adopted 16 December 1966, in force 23 March 1976. Binding.\n• ICCPR: which rights does it cover: Civil and political rights: life, no torture, fair trial, expression (Art. 19), religion (Art. 18), assembly, elections."],
  ["hr","ICESCR: dates, rights, and why two covenants?","• ICESCR: adopted and in force when: Adopted 16 December 1966, in force 3 January 1976. Binding.\n• ICESCR: which rights and what standard: Work, social security, health, education, food, housing. \"Progressive realization\" within available resources. Optional Protocol (2008) for complaints.\n• Why are there two covenants: Both were adopted together by A/RES/2200 A (XXI), split along Cold War lines: the West stressed civil and political rights, the East economic and social rights."],
  ["hr","What is the International Bill of Human Rights?","The UDHR (1948), ICCPR and ICESCR (both 1966)."],
  ["hr","HRC: how and when was it created?","A/RES/60/251 on 15 March 2006, as a subsidiary organ of the GA. First session June 2006, in Geneva."],
  ["hr","What did the HRC replace, and why?","The Commission on Human Rights (an ECOSOC commission, 1946–2006), criticized for politicization and for abusive states sitting on it."],
  ["hr","HRC membership: size and election?","47 members, elected by the GA by absolute majority (97 votes) in a secret ballot, for three-year terms; no more than two consecutive terms. Candidates' human rights records are supposed to be taken into account."],
  ["hr","HRC seats by region?","Africa 13, Asia-Pacific 13, Latin America and Caribbean 8, Western Europe and Others 7, Eastern Europe 6."],
  ["hr","Which states have been suspended from the HRC?","By a two-thirds GA vote for gross and systematic violations: Libya (2011), Russia (April 2022)."],
  ["hr","Are HRC resolutions binding, and what did the US do in 2025?","• Are HRC resolutions binding: No. Its power is scrutiny, investigation, naming and standard-setting.\n• What did the United States do regarding the HRC in 2025: It withdrew from the Council again."],
  ["hr","What is OHCHR and when was it created?","The Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights, created by A/RES/48/141 (December 1993) following the Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action (June 1993)."],
  ["hr","What did the 1993 Vienna Declaration say about rights?","It declared all rights \"universal, indivisible and interdependent\"."],
  ["hr","What does OHCHR do?","It is the HRC's secretariat and supports treaty bodies, field offices and technical cooperation."],
  ["hr","Who is the High Commissioner for Human Rights?","Volker Türk (Austria) since October 2022."],
  ["hr","In 30 seconds: what is the Human Rights Council?","A 47-member GA subsidiary body in Geneva, created in 2006 to replace the discredited Commission on Human Rights. It cannot bind states but scrutinizes, investigates and sets standards, supported by OHCHR under Volker Türk."],
  ["r2p","What is the Responsibility to Protect (R2P)?","The political commitment that sovereignty is a responsibility, not just a right: states must protect their populations from four atrocity crimes, and if they manifestly fail, the international community should act."],
  ["r2p","What failures led to R2P?","Rwanda (1994: about 800,000 people, mostly Tutsi, killed in about 100 days while a small UN mission was cut back) and Srebrenica (1995: about 8,000 Bosniak men and boys killed in a declared UN \"safe area\")."],
  ["r2p","When was R2P endorsed, and where?","Unanimously at the 2005 World Summit, paragraphs 138–139 of the World Summit Outcome (A/RES/60/1)."],
  ["r2p","Which four crimes does R2P cover?","Genocide, war crimes, ethnic cleansing, crimes against humanity. Nothing else (not natural disasters, not general human rights abuses)."],
  ["r2p","R2P pillars one, two and three?","• R2P pillar one: The state's own responsibility to protect its population.\n• R2P pillar two: The international community's responsibility to assist states in doing so (capacity-building, early warning).\n• R2P pillar three: When a state is manifestly failing, the international community must take timely and decisive action through the UN Charter: peaceful means first, and force only through the Security Council."],
  ["r2p","Is R2P a binding treaty?","No. A political commitment. The underlying crimes are prohibited by law; R2P is about responding to them."],
  ["r2p","First major use of R2P?","Libya (2011), where force was authorized to protect civilians."],
  ["r2p","Why is pillar three contested after Libya?","NATO's campaign ended in regime change, which made Russia, China and many Global South states suspicious."],
  ["r2p","What did Syria show about R2P?","The limits: repeated vetoes blocked action."],
  ["r2p","France–Mexico initiative and the ACT Code of Conduct?","• What is the France–Mexico initiative (2015): It asks the P5 to voluntarily not veto in mass-atrocity situations.\n• What is the ACT Group Code of Conduct (2015): It asks all SC members not to vote against credible action to prevent atrocities, with 120+ supporters."],
  ["r2p","How would you answer an interview question on R2P?","\"R2P reframes sovereignty as responsibility. It is strongest on pillars one and two, prevention and assistance, which most states support; pillar three remains contested after Libya, which is why many delegations prefer prevention and early warning.\""],
  ["peace","Is peacekeeping in the Charter?","No. It is not mentioned in the Charter."],
  ["peace","First UN peacekeeping missions?","UNTSO (1948, Middle East, unarmed observers) and UNMOGIP (1949, India–Pakistan). Both are still running."],
  ["peace","Peacekeeping by the numbers?","• Peacekeeping by the numbers: Nobel Peace Prize 1988. Over 70 operations since 1948. More than 4,000 peacekeepers have died in service.\n• How many peacekeeping operations and countries: More than 70 operations. Hundreds of thousands of military personnel, plus tens of thousands of police and civilians from more than 120 countries, have taken part."],
  ["peace","The three principles of peacekeeping?","Consent of the parties, impartiality, non-use of force except in self-defence and defence of the mandate."],
  ["peace","Does the UN have its own army?","No. States volunteer troops and police. Peacekeepers wear the blue helmet or beret to show UN neutrality."],
  ["peace","Largest troop contributors to peacekeeping?","Nepal, Bangladesh, India, Rwanda, Pakistan (Egypt is among the top contributors; Ghana also appears)."],
  ["peace","How is peacekeeping funded?","By a separate assessed budget; the largest payers are the US and China. The budget and number of missions have shrunk since 2023."],
  ["peace","Which agendas relate to peacekeeping?","Women, Peace and Security (S/RES/1325); Youth, Peace and Security (S/RES/2250); SDG 16; A New Agenda for Peace (2023); the Pact for the Future (2024)."],
  ["peace","Do peacekeepers wear UN uniforms?","No. Blue helmets and berets signal neutrality and UN authority; soldiers keep their national uniforms and weapons, supplied by their governments."],
  ["peace","How are peacekeepers recruited?","Uniformed personnel are nominated by their governments (you can't apply individually). Civilians are recruited directly by the UN or through UN Volunteers."],
  ["peace","Which peacekeeping missions have been deadliest?","ONUC, UNIFIL and MINUSMA. Attacks on peacekeepers can be war crimes."],
  ["peace","Who won a Nobel Peace Prize for peacekeeping?","UN peacekeeping won it in 1988; Lester Pearson won in 1957 for UNEF."],
  ["peace","Preventive diplomacy, peacemaking, peacekeeping, peace enforcement, peacebuilding, special political missions?","• Preventive diplomacy: Action to stop disputes escalating: envoys, good offices, early warning.\n• Peacemaking: Bringing parties to agreement, mainly by negotiation and mediation (Chapter VI).\n• Peacekeeping: Deployed presence with consent to help implement a ceasefire or peace agreement.\n• Peace enforcement: Coercive force authorized under Chapter VII, possibly without consent (e.g. Korea, Kuwait, or AU forces in Somalia).\n• Peacebuilding: Long-term work on the causes of conflict: institutions, rule of law, economy, reconciliation.\n• Special political missions: Civilian missions run by DPPA (UNAMA in Afghanistan, UNSMIL in Libya, envoy offices)."],
  ["peace","In 30 seconds: what is peacekeeping?","Not in the Charter; it grew out of practice from 1948. The Security Council authorizes missions staffed by volunteered national troops and police under three principles: consent, impartiality, and no force except self-defence and defence of the mandate. Funded by a separate assessed budget."],
  ["dev","What were the Millennium Development Goals?","Eight goals, 2000–2015, from the Millennium Declaration."],
  ["dev","What is the 2030 Agenda?","A/RES/70/1, adopted 25 September 2015. 17 goals, 169 targets, universal, \"leave no one behind\"."],
  ["dev","UNFCCC, Kyoto, Paris Agreement: key facts, NDCs and the US?","• UNFCCC: key facts: Opened at the Rio Earth Summit (1992); 198 parties. Goal: prevent \"dangerous\" human interference with the climate. The annual COP is its supreme body; secretariat in Bonn.\n• Kyoto Protocol: 1997, in force 2005; binding cuts for developed countries only.\n• Paris Agreement: when and what: Adopted 12 December 2015 at COP21, in force 4 November 2016. Keep warming \"well below 2°C\" and pursue 1.5°C.\n• What must every party to the Paris Agreement submit: Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) every five years.\n• What happened with the US and the Paris Agreement: The US withdrawal took effect in January 2026."],
  ["dev","MDGs vs SDGs: what changed?","MDGs: eight goals, 2000–2015. SDGs: 17 goals and 169 targets, universal (apply to all countries), 2015–2030."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: capital and where?","Maputo, in the far south next to South Africa and Eswatini."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: population?","Almost 37 million, very young (median age about 17)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: languages?","Portuguese (official) plus Emakhuwa, Xichangana and other Bantu languages."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: religion?","Christian majority; about one in five Muslim, concentrated in the north, including the Cabo Delgado coast."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: independence?","25 June 1975, from Portugal."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: system of government?","Presidential republic. FRELIMO has governed since independence."],
  ["moz","What is FRELIMO and its history in three stages?","• What is FRELIMO: The Mozambique Liberation Front, the main political party, founded in 1962 by Eduardo Mondlane (assassinated in 1969) and led after him by Samora Machel (first president of Mozambique).\n• FRELIMO's history in three stages: An anti-colonial guerrilla movement (1964–1974 war against Portuguese rule); a Marxist–Leninist single-party state from 1975 to 1989; then a shift in the 1990s toward a multi-party system and democratic socialism."],
  ["moz","Who is Mozambique's President?","Daniel Chapo (FRELIMO), sworn in 15 January 2025; the first president born after independence."],
  ["moz","Who is Mozambique's foreign minister?","Maria Manuela dos Santos Lucas, since January 2025; the first career diplomat in the post. [check news]"],
  ["moz","Who is Mozambique's UN Permanent Representative?","Domingos Estêvão Fernandes (credentials March 2025). [check news]"],
  ["moz","Mozambique's development status?","Least developed country (LDC); near the bottom of the Human Development Index; GDP per person about $660."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: GDP, GDP per person, recent growth?","• Mozambique: total GDP: About $23.6 billion (2025). Roughly a twentieth of South Africa's economy.\n• Mozambique: GDP per person: About $660 (2025), one of the lowest in the world.\n• Mozambique: growth in recent years: 5.5% in 2023, 2.2% in 2024, then a contraction of 0.2% in 2025 after the post-election unrest, cyclones and the Mozal closure. Forecast 0.6% in 2026, rising to 2.5% by 2028 as LNG construction resumes."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: poverty, HDI rank and public debt?","• Mozambique: extreme poverty: About 82% live on under $3 a day (World Bank, 2025 estimate), around 26–27 million people. By the national poverty line, poverty rose from 48% (2014/15) to 63% (2022).\n• Mozambique: Human Development Index: Ranked 182nd of 193 (2025 report, value 0.493); \"low human development\".\n• Mozambique: public debt: About 91% of GDP (2025), forecast to pass 100% in 2027. The IMF and World Bank rate it \"in distress\"; the hidden debts are a big reason."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: currency?","Metical (MZN)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: main exports?","Coal and natural gas (about $2 billion each in 2024), then aluminium (falling after Mozal's closure), heavy sands, rubies, electricity, sugar and cashews."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: life expectancy?","About 64 years."],
  ["moz","How do poverty, debt and climate shocks link to Mozambique's positions?","They explain why it asks for development finance, debt relief and burden-sharing (UNHCR), and why it frames the Cabo Delgado insurgency as rooted in exclusion from resource wealth (GA1, PMSCs)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: coastline?","About 2,500 km on the Indian Ocean and the Mozambique Channel (between it and Madagascar), a major shipping lane between Asia and the Atlantic."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: land neighbours?","Six: Tanzania (north), Malawi, Zambia, Zimbabwe (west), South Africa and Eswatini (south). Mnemonic: \"TMZ-ZSE\"."],
  ["moz","Why is Mozambique a gateway for landlocked neighbours?","Its ports at Maputo, Beira and Nacala sit at the end of transport corridors for Malawi, Zambia and Zimbabwe, making it strategically important to all of southern Africa."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: great rivers and the Cahora Bassa dam?","The Zambezi and Limpopo. The Cahora Bassa dam on the Zambezi exports electricity to South Africa."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: key resources?","Offshore Rovuma Basin gas off Cabo Delgado (among the largest in Africa), coal at Moatize (Tete), the world's largest ruby deposit at Montepuez, graphite at Balama, heavy sands, and the Mozal aluminium smelter near Maputo."],
  ["moz","What happened to Mozal?","On care and maintenance since March 2026, after South32 could not agree an affordable power deal; about 4,000 jobs lost."],
  ["moz","Mozambique: climate exposure?","One of the most cyclone- and flood-prone countries in the world: Idai and Kenneth (2019), Freddy (2023), Chido (December 2024, hit Cabo Delgado), Dikeledi and Jude (2025). Disasters regularly displace hundreds of thousands, on top of the conflict."],
  ["moz","When and where was FRELIMO founded?","1962 in Tanzania, under Eduardo Mondlane (assassinated 1969). Armed liberation struggle from 1964."],
  ["moz","How did Mozambique reach independence?","25 June 1975, after Portugal's 1974 Carnation Revolution and the Lusaka Accord. First president Samora Machel (killed in a 1986 plane crash in South Africa)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's civil war?","1977–1992 between the FRELIMO government and RENAMO, backed first by Rhodesia, then by apartheid South Africa. About a million dead and millions displaced, including over 1.5 million refugees in neighbouring countries."],
  ["moz","How did Mozambique's civil war end?","The Rome General Peace Accords, 4 October 1992, mediated by the Catholic Community of Sant'Egidio."],
  ["moz","ONUMOZ and renewed RENAMO fighting (to the Maputo Accord)?","• What did ONUMOZ do: The UN peacekeeping mission (1992–94) oversaw demobilisation and the 1994 elections. Often cited as a UN success.\n• Renewed RENAMO fighting: 2013–2019, ended by the Maputo Accord (6 August 2019). Disarmament of RENAMO fighters completed in 2023."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's history in six steps?","1. Portuguese colony from the 1500s. 2. FRELIMO founded 1962, armed struggle from 1964. 3. Independence 1975. 4. Civil war 1977–1992. 5. Rome Accords 1992 and ONUMOZ. 6. Renewed fighting 2013–19, ended by the Maputo Accord."],
  ["moz","Cabo Delgado insurgency: start, roots and key moments?","• Cabo Delgado insurgency: when and who: Since 5 October 2017. An Islamist armed group, locally called \"al-Shabaab\" (no link to Somalia's) and affiliated with the Islamic State, attacked Mocímboa da Praia.\n• Cabo Delgado insurgency: what are the roots: Poverty, youth unemployment, marginalisation of the Muslim coastal population, and exclusion from the province's gas and ruby wealth.\n• Cabo Delgado: key moments: Mocímboa seized August 2020. The Palma attack of March 2021 next to the gas site. Mocímboa retaken by Rwandan and Mozambican forces in August 2021."],
  ["moz","Cabo Delgado: casualties, current state and displacement?","• Cabo Delgado: casualties and current state: Over 6,000 killed. Attacks continue in southern districts such as Ancuabe and Chiúre in 2025–26. [check news]\n• Cabo Delgado displacement numbers: UNHCR counts over 1.3 million people displaced at some point since 2017. IOM counted over 345,000 still displaced at the end of 2025; nearly 100,000 fled a new wave of violence in late 2025. About 900,000 people in Cabo Delgado need humanitarian aid. Roughly 80% of the newly displaced are women and children. [check news]"],
  ["moz","Foreign security help in Cabo Delgado: contractors and states?","• Foreign security help in Cabo Delgado: private contractors: Wagner Group (from September 2019, withdrew after losses within months), then South Africa's Dyck Advisory Group (April 2020 to April 2021).\n• Foreign security help in Cabo Delgado: states: Rwanda (bilateral deployment from July 2021, partly financed by the EU's European Peace Facility), the SADC Mission in Mozambique (SAMIM) July 2021 to July 2024, EU training missions (EUTM, now EUMAM Mozambique)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique LNG and other gas projects?","• The Mozambique LNG project: TotalEnergies' $20.5 billion project declared force majeure after the Palma attack (April 2021). Lifted in November 2025; full restart announced January 2026; first LNG targeted for 2029.\n• Other gas projects in Mozambique: Eni's Coral South floating plant has exported since 2022; ExxonMobil's Rovuma LNG is next."],
  ["moz","What are the allegations about soldiers guarding the LNG site?","A 2024 Politico investigation alleged abuses by soldiers guarding the site in 2021. Know it exists, don't volunteer it."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's 2024 post-election crisis and how it eased?","• Mozambique's 2024 post-election crisis: After the 9 October 2024 elections Chapo was declared winner (about 65% after the Constitutional Council's ruling). Opposition figure Venâncio Mondlane led months of protests; rights groups count about 300–400 killed by security forces, with internet restrictions and the South African border temporarily closed.\n• How did the 2024 crisis ease: Chapo and Mondlane met in March 2025 and a national dialogue process followed."],
  ["moz","The \"hidden debts\" scandal?","2013–2016: about $2 billion in secret state-guaranteed loans (via Credit Suisse and VTB) to three state companies (ProIndicus, EMATUM, MAM). Exposed in 2016; the IMF and donors suspended support and the economy crashed."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's neighbour issues?","No major active territorial disputes. Frictions: Tanzania forcibly returned over 10,000 Mozambicans fleeing Cabo Delgado in 2021 (UNHCR protested); Malawi's wish to navigate the Shire–Zambezi waterway to the sea; tensions with South Africa over border closures and xenophobic violence against Mozambican migrants."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's foreign policy principles: what does each mean?","• Mozambique's foreign policy: non-alignment: \"Friends with all\": close historic ties with Russia and China (Soviet-era support for FRELIMO; Russian FM Lavrov visited Maputo in July 2026), while working closely with the EU, the US, Portugal and Rwanda.\n• Mozambique's foreign policy: African solutions: A founding member of SADC (via SADCC, 1980) and a strong believer in regional security cooperation, shown by SAMIM.\n• Mozambique's foreign policy: sovereignty first: It invites help on its terms (Rwanda, SADC, the EU) and resists outside conditions.\n• Mozambique's foreign policy: peace through dialogue: It is proud of the 1992 and 2019 peace accords and of ONUMOZ, so it argues for political solutions, reconciliation and reintegration.\n• Mozambique's foreign policy: development is security: The official line on Cabo Delgado pairs military action with jobs, services and the Northern Integrated Development Agency (ADIN, created 2020)."],
  ["moz","How did Mozambique vote on the 2022 GA resolution on Russia's invasion of Ukraine?","It abstained."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's UN regional group and groupings?","• Mozambique's UN regional group: African States.\n• Mozambique's groupings: African Union, SADC, the Group of 77 and China, Non-Aligned Movement, LDCs, Commonwealth (1995; the first member never ruled by Britain), CPLP (founding member 1996), the Organisation of Islamic Cooperation, Indian Ocean Rim Association."],
  ["moz","Mozambique on the Security Council and peacekeeping history?","• Mozambique on the Security Council: Elected member 2023–2024, its first term ever. Priorities included counter-terrorism, women, peace and security, and African peace operations.\n• Mozambique and peacekeeping history: It hosted ONUMOZ (1992–94), one of the UN's peacekeeping success stories."],
  ["moz","Mozambique's disarmament credentials and the key treaty it is NOT party to?","• Mozambique's disarmament credentials: Declared free of known landmines in 2015 after decades of clearance. Party to the Mine Ban Treaty, the Convention on Cluster Munitions (2011), the Chemical Weapons Convention (2000), the NPT and the Treaty of Pelindaba.\n• Which key treaty is Mozambique NOT party to on autonomous weapons: The CCW (the Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons, where autonomous weapons are negotiated)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique and refugee law?","1951 Refugee Convention (acceded 1983, with reservations on several articles, including freedom of movement), the 1967 Protocol, the OAU Refugee Convention (1969), the Kampala Convention (ratified 2017), and its own Policy and Strategy for the Management of Internally Displaced Persons (2021)."],
  ["moz","Mozambique and private military law?","Not a participant in the Montreux Document and not a party to the 1989 UN Mercenaries Convention (per Mozambican civil society, 2020)."],
  ["moz","Model answer: how should you sound like Mozambique?","\"Mozambique speaks from experience. We have lived through a liberation war, a civil war, a peace process the UN helped deliver, and since 2017 an insurgency in Cabo Delgado that has displaced over a million of our people. That is why we believe security, accountability and development cannot be separated.\""],
  ["moz","Three themes to repeat in both committees as Mozambique?","Lived experience (we know this first-hand); African and regional ownership (SADC, the AU, partnership on our terms); capacity and fair burden-sharing (frameworks must come with the means for developing states to implement them)."],
  ["mozrecord","How does Mozambique open UN statements, and what formula do you use?","• How does Mozambique open most UN statements: It aligns with groups: \"Mozambique aligns itself with the statements delivered on behalf of the African Group, the Non-Aligned Movement, and the Group of 77 and China\" (sometimes the CPLP), \"and wishes to add the following remarks in its national capacity\".\n• What does Mozambique add after aligning with groups: National experience: landmines, the civil war, Cabo Delgado, cyclones, refugees.\n• What formula should you use in the interview: \"Mozambique aligns with the African Group, and in its national capacity stresses...\""],
  ["mozrecord","Mozambique's recurring themes at the UN?","Disarmament is a development issue, capacity-building for developing countries, African and regional frameworks, and multilateralism."],
  ["mozrecord","GA1, Oct 2025: landmines and small arms?","• GA1, 23 October 2025: landmines: \"This year, Mozambique marks ten years since being declared landmine-free, a national achievement built upon global solidarity and a reminder of the enduring human cost of conventional weapons.\"\n• GA1, October 2025: small arms: Producing and trafficking weapons is cheap; destroying them and rehabilitating communities is \"prohibitively high, particularly for developing countries. Disarmament, therefore, is not only a security goal, it is a development imperative.\"\n• GA1, October 2025: small arms commitments: Full commitment to the UN Programme of Action and the International Tracing Instrument; strong support for a UN Fellowship Programme on small arms for developing countries (an African Group priority); links to the SADC Protocol, the Nairobi and Bamako Declarations and \"Silencing the Guns in Africa\"."],
  ["mozrecord","GA1, October 2025: Mozambique on autonomous weapons?","\"A dangerous convergence between conventional weapons and emerging technologies, including autonomous systems, armed drones, and artificial intelligence-enabled targeting.\" These \"low-cost innovations magnify lethality and accessibility while escaping traditional arms-control regimes.\" It supports renewed global dialogue and \"the need to preserve human control and accountability over all uses of force.\""],
  ["mozrecord","Other WMD debate, Oct 2025: what did Mozambique warn and urge?","• Other WMD debate, 22 October 2025: what did Mozambique warn about: The convergence of terrorism, WMD and emerging technology (\"AI-driven biodesign, 3D printing, and unregulated drone technology\") and the threat from non-State actors.\n• What did Mozambique urge on resolution 1540: Full implementation. During its SC term it co-hosted with UNODA and the 1540 Committee the 3rd Training Course for National Focal Points in Africa (Addis Ababa, 5–7 November 2024, 42 states), praised by High Representative Nakamitsu.\n• What did Mozambique call for on WMD: A legally binding verification mechanism for the Biological Weapons Convention, African centres of excellence on CBRN risks, and \"algorithmic accountability and AI safeguards\" in future WMD rules \"to prevent the automation of destruction\"."],
  ["mozrecord","How did Mozambique vote on A/RES/80/57 and A/RES/80/194?","• How did Mozambique vote on A/RES/80/57: Yes. Lethal autonomous weapons systems, 1 December 2025, adopted 164–6–7.\n• How did Mozambique vote on A/RES/80/194: Yes. Use of mercenaries as a means of violating human rights and impeding self-determination, 15 December 2025 (Third Committee), 121–53–5; Western states voted against."],
  ["mozrecord","Mozambique at the UNHCR Executive Committee?","• Mozambique and the UNHCR Executive Committee: Member (applied 1998). Ambassador Pedro Comissário Afonso chaired ExCom in 2014–15 and opened its 66th session in October 2015.\n• Mozambique at ExCom each year: It speaks every year, usually aligned with the African Group.\n• Mozambique at ExCom: 2021 and 2022: 2021: the INAR Director-General pressed for an out-of-camp refugee policy and asked UNHCR for guidance on it. 2022: aligned with the African Group statement.\n• Mozambique at ExCom: 2024: Ambassador Geraldo Saranga reported about 26,000 refugees and asylum seekers, called for more funding for refugees, and highlighted the completed national statelessness study with UNHCR."],
  ["mozrecord","Global Refugee Forum pledges and Mozambique's legal steps?","• Mozambique and the Global Refugee Forum (2019 pledges): UNHCR says it made significant progress: it built a secondary school in Maratane, now enrolling more refugee children, and signed a memorandum of understanding with UNHCR.\n• Mozambique and the 2023 Global Refugee Forum: It pledged to continue local integration, build synergies between refugees and host communities, and increase access to education, health and basic services. UNHCR's representative said Mozambique is \"setting an example for inclusion, even as global support weakens\".\n• Mozambique's legal steps on refugees and statelessness: Kampala Convention ratified (2017), statelessness conventions acceded (2014), national IDP policy (2021)."],
  ["mozrecord","Mozambique's Security Council presidencies and why they matter?","• Mozambique on the Security Council: how many times did it preside: Twice, in its first term (2023–2024).\n• Mozambique's March 2023 Council presidency: Foreign Minister Verónica Macamo chaired an open debate on women, peace and security; President Filipe Nyusi chaired a high-level debate on counter-terrorism.\n• Mozambique's May 2024 Council presidency: The annual protection of civilians open debate (25th anniversary of resolution 1265), an open debate on the role of African states in addressing global security and development challenges (with a presidential statement), and a debate on the role of women and young people in peace and security.\n• Why does Mozambique's Council term matter for your committees: Protection of civilians links to both (contractors in conflict; displacement), and it shows Mozambique as a credible voice on security."],
  ["mozrecord","Mozambique's real line on LAWS, PMSCs, psychosocial support and migration routes?","• Mozambique's real line on autonomous weapons: \"Preserve human control and accountability over all uses of force\"; voted yes on A/RES/80/57; worried about low-cost technologies reaching non-State actors.\n• Mozambique's real line on private military companies: Voted yes on the mercenaries resolution; its own experience with Wagner and DAG; aligns with the African Group's call for binding regulation.\n• Mozambique's real line on psychosocial support: Inclusion and local integration; access to health and education for refugees and host communities (its 2023 pledge).\n• Mozambique's real line on migration routes: More funding and burden-sharing; regional (SADC, AU) frameworks; its own citizens were refouled by Tanzania in 2021."],
  ["ga1","What is the full name of GA1?","First Committee of the General Assembly: the Disarmament and International Security Committee (DISEC)."],
  ["ga1","List the six Main Committees of the GA.","First: disarmament. Second: economic and financial. Third: social, humanitarian and cultural. Fourth: special political and decolonisation. Fifth: administrative and budgetary. Sixth: legal."],
  ["ga1","GA1 membership, voting, location and timing?","• GA1: who are the members and how do they vote: All 193 member states, one vote each. Observers (the Holy See, Palestine) and the EU can speak but not vote.\n• GA1: where and when does it meet: New York, every autumn, roughly early October to early November (4–5 weeks)."],
  ["ga1","Who supports GA1?","UNODA for substance; the Department for General Assembly and Conference Management for meetings."],
  ["ga1","GA1: how many drafts a year, and where do they go?","About 60–70 draft resolutions and decisions, sent to the GA plenary for final adoption in early December."],
  ["ga1","Is GA1's output binding?","No. Recommendations only."],
  ["ga1","Who chairs the 81st GA1 and how did GA1 begin?","• Who chairs the 81st session of GA1 (2026): Ambassador Lok Bahadur Thapa (Nepal). Bureau posts rotate among the five regional groups.\n• How did GA1 begin: Created 1945–46 as the Political and Security Committee, handling all political and security items, including disarmament."],
  ["ga1","Who opened the 81st session and when?","High Representative for Disarmament Affairs Izumi Nakamitsu (Japan, in post since 2017), on 6 October 2026."],
  ["ga1","First GA resolution ever and what changed for GA1 in 1978?","• First GA resolution ever: 1(I), January 1946: set up the UN Atomic Energy Commission. Disarmament was on the agenda from day one.\n• What changed for GA1 in 1978: After the first Special Session on Disarmament (SSOD-I) it was limited to disarmament and related international security questions. Other political items went to the Special Political Committee (merged with the Fourth in 1993)."],
  ["ga1","GA1's mandate in one line?","Disarmament, global challenges and threats to peace affecting the international community; all disarmament and international security matters within the Charter."],
  ["ga1","Charter Articles 11, 12, 13 and 26 for GA1?","• Charter Article 11(1) for GA1: The GA may consider the general principles of co-operation in maintaining peace and security, including disarmament and arms regulation, and may make recommendations.\n• Charter Article 11(2): The GA may discuss any peace and security question brought by a member, the Security Council or a non-member.\n• Charter Article 12 for GA1: The GA may not recommend on a dispute the Security Council is currently handling (unless the Council asks).\n• Charter Article 26 for GA1: The Security Council is responsible for plans to regulate armaments. GA1 sets norms; the Council acts.\n• Charter Article 13 for GA1: The GA encourages the progressive development and codification of international law, so GA1 can launch treaty work."],
  ["ga1","Does GA1 concern a particular group of people?","No: all states, increasingly human security (civilians, victims, survivors)."],
  ["ga1","What can GA1 do?","Debate and recommend; request reports from the SG; create bodies and processes (GGEs, OEWGs); launch and recommend treaties; convene conferences and special sessions."],
  ["ga1","Example of GA1 requesting a report?","A/RES/78/241 on autonomous weapons led to the SG report A/79/88 (2024)."],
  ["ga1","GGE vs OEWG?","A Group of Governmental Experts has a limited number of experts; an Open-Ended Working Group is open to all states."],
  ["ga1","Three treaties launched through GA1?","Arms Trade Treaty (process launched 2006, adopted by the GA 2013); Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons (GA mandate, 2017); CTBT (adopted by the GA 1996)."],
  ["ga1","The Middle East WMD-free zone conference?","Held annually since 2019 under a GA mandate. Israel and the US do not take part."],
  ["ga1","What GA1 cannot do?","Bind states, impose sanctions or embargoes, authorise force, deploy missions or inspect facilities. Those belong to the Security Council and treaty bodies like the IAEA and OPCW."],
  ["ga1","GA1 session phases and the seven thematic clusters?","• Phases of a GA1 session: 1) General debate (about 8 meetings). 2) Thematic discussion (about 2 weeks, seven clusters, with panels). 3) Action on all drafts (about 1 week): votes, explanations of vote, rights of reply.\n• The seven thematic clusters of GA1: Nuclear weapons; other WMD; outer space (disarmament aspects); conventional weapons; other disarmament measures and international security (ICT, AI, gender, development); regional disarmament and security; disarmament machinery."],
  ["ga1","Which cluster holds both your topics?","Conventional weapons (private military companies and autonomous weapons, plus small arms, landmines, cluster munitions, ammunition)."],
  ["ga1","Typical main sponsors in GA1?","Austria on autonomous weapons, Japan on nuclear disarmament, Mexico and Ireland on humanitarian disarmament, the NAM on many nuclear texts."],
  ["ga1","\"Biennialised\" resolutions?","Taken up every two (or three) years to save time."],
  ["ga1","How GA1 decides: majority, recorded votes, and the plenary majority?","• How does GA1 decide: Simple majority of members present and voting (abstentions do not count). Many texts pass without a vote, but the share is shrinking.\n• What can states request in a vote: Recorded votes, separate votes on paragraphs, and explanations of vote.\n• What majority does the plenary need on peace and security recommendations: Two-thirds (Charter Article 18(2) \"important questions\")."],
  ["ga1","Where do budget implications of a new process go?","To the Fifth Committee before plenary adoption."],
  ["ga1","What did Nakamitsu say about voting in the 81st session?","That \"increasing numbers of votes and rights of reply\" show a more divided committee."],
  ["ga1","Disarmament machinery: SSOD-I, UNDC, CD and its agenda?","• What three-part machinery did SSOD-I (1978) create: A deliberative body (UNDC), a negotiating body (CD) and the First Committee itself.\n• UN Disarmament Commission (UNDC): GA subsidiary, deliberative; all 193 members; about three weeks each spring in New York; works by consensus on guidelines.\n• Conference on Disarmament (CD): Autonomous negotiating forum in Geneva; 65 members; consensus rule; negotiated the CWC (1992) and CTBT (1996); deadlocked since 1996, unable to agree a programme of work.\n• What is on the CD's agenda: A fissile material cut-off treaty and prevention of an arms race in outer space (PAROS)."],
  ["ga1","UNODA and UNIDIR?","• UNODA: Secretariat office led by High Representative Izumi Nakamitsu; offices in New York, Geneva and Vienna.\n• UNIDIR: UN Institute for Disarmament Research, Geneva, 1980; autonomous; leading research on AI, autonomy, cyber and arms control."],
  ["ga1","Recent GA1 working groups and the Scientific Panel on nuclear war?","• Recent GA1 OEWG on ICT security: 2021–2025; led to a permanent Global Mechanism on ICT security agreed by consensus in 2025.\n• Recent GA1 OEWG on conventional ammunition: Led to the 2023 Global Framework for Through-life Conventional Ammunition Management.\n• Independent Scientific Panel on the Effects of Nuclear War: Created by a recent GA1 resolution; will publish the first independent study since 1989 in summer 2027."],
  ["ga1","NPT, IAEA, CWC/OPCW, BWC, CTBT, TPNW: key facts?","• NPT: key facts: 1968 (in force 1970). Three pillars: disarmament, non-proliferation, peaceful use. Review Conferences every five years; 2015, 2022 and 2026 all failed to agree an outcome.\n• IAEA: 1957, Vienna. Verifies non-proliferation; reports to the GA and, when needed, the Security Council.\n• CWC and OPCW: 1993 (in force 1997), The Hague. Mozambique joined in 2000.\n• Biological Weapons Convention: 1972. No verification body; a small Implementation Support Unit in Geneva.\n• CTBT / CTBTO: 1996, Vienna. Not in force: needs ratification by specific states including the US, China, India, Pakistan, Iran, Egypt, Israel and North Korea.\n• TPNW: Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons, 2017 (in force 2021). First Review Conference December 2026; membership growing."],
  ["ga1","Mine Ban, Cluster Munitions, CCW, ATT, Programme of Action, Pelindaba, resolution 1540?","• Mine Ban Treaty: Ottawa, 1997. Mozambique is a party and was declared mine-free in 2015. Several European states announced withdrawals in 2025.\n• Convention on Cluster Munitions: 2008. Mozambique ratified 2011; Lithuania withdrew in 2025.\n• CCW: Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons, 1980, Geneva. Home of the autonomous weapons talks. Mozambique is not a party.\n• Arms Trade Treaty: 2013 (in force 2014). Regulates international transfers of conventional arms.\n• UN Programme of Action on small arms: 2001, plus the International Tracing Instrument (2005). Africa's biggest disarmament issue.\n• Treaty of Pelindaba: 1996 (in force 2009). Makes Africa a nuclear-weapon-free zone; Mozambique is a party.\n• Security Council resolution 1540: 2004. Binding duty to stop weapons of mass destruction reaching non-state actors; overseen by the 1540 Committee."],
  ["ga1","How does the Security Council relate to GA1?","Binding arms embargoes, sanctions, the 1540 regime and DDR mandates in peacekeeping."],
  ["ga1","Where do outer space matters go?","Peaceful uses to the Fourth Committee; GA1 covers the military side."],
  ["ga1","Which HRC bodies relate to GA1 topics?","The intergovernmental working group on private military and security companies and the Working Group on the use of mercenaries."],
  ["ga1","DDR and the Mozambique link?","Disarmament, demobilisation and reintegration of fighters: in Mozambique with RENAMO, completed 2023."],
  ["ga1","Which two SG documents matter for GA1?","The 2018 disarmament agenda \"Securing Our Common Future\" and the 2023 New Agenda for Peace (which called for a treaty on autonomous weapons by 2026)."],
  ["ga1","Nakamitsu's three challenges: nuclear risk, spending, military AI?","• Nakamitsu's three main challenges (6 Oct 2026): Nuclear risk higher than any time since the Cold War; record military spending; military AI.\n• Nuclear arms control in 2026: New START has expired: for the first time in fifty years there are no caps on the two largest arsenals. Three NPT Review Conferences in a row have failed.\n• Military AI at GA1: Informal exchanges on AI in the military domain, created by GA1, have begun; Nakamitsu called for a dedicated multilateral forum and noted agreed elements for an instrument on lethal autonomous weapons as a recent success."],
  ["ga1","How is GA1 funded, and what squeezes disarmament work?","• How is GA1 funded: No budget of its own: meetings are paid from the UN regular budget. UNODA is partly regular budget, heavily voluntary contributions and trust funds.\n• What squeezes disarmament work: The UN liquidity crisis and the UN80 reform initiative (2025); new processes need Fifth Committee approval."],
  ["ga1","Criticisms of GA1: polarisation, talking shop, nuclear divide, withdrawals, consensus veto?","• Criticism: polarisation: More recorded votes, rival resolutions (e.g. competing outer space texts from Russia and from Western states) and rights of reply.\n• Criticism: \"talking shop\": GA1 cannot bind anyone, and the CD it relies on has been stuck since 1996.\n• Criticism: nuclear divide: TPNW supporters (most of the Global South, including Africa) versus nuclear-armed states and allies, which reject it.\n• Criticism: treaty withdrawals: European states leaving the landmine and cluster munition treaties, criticised as reversing humanitarian progress.\n• Criticism: consensus as a veto: In the CCW and CD one state can block everything, which is why many push to move autonomous weapons talks to the GA."],
  ["ga1","Mozambique in GA1: alignment, Operation Rachel, small arms commitments?","• Mozambique in GA1: whom does it align with: The African Group, NAM and G77 and China (October 2025 statement on conventional weapons aligned with all three).\n• Operation Rachel: Joint South Africa–Mozambique police operation from 1995 that found and destroyed weapons caches from the civil war; a regional model.\n• Mozambique's small arms commitments: Bound by the SADC Protocol on Firearms (2001); backs the AU's \"Silencing the Guns\" agenda."],
  ["ga1","Why did you choose GA1 for Mozambique?","\"Its topics are not theoretical for us. They cleared their country of landmines, destroyed the weapons left by their civil war, and in Cabo Delgado hired private military companies and saw both the accountability gaps and the limits of outsourcing security. GA1 is where all 193 states, including small ones like Mozambique, shape the rules on those questions.\""],
  ["pmsc","What is a PMSC (Montreux definition and services)?","• Montreux Document definition of a PMSC: \"Private business entities that provide military and/or security services, irrespective of how they describe themselves.\"\n• What services do PMSCs provide: Armed guarding and protection of people and objects, maintaining and operating weapons systems, detaining prisoners, advising or training armed forces."],
  ["pmsc","Mercenary legal definition and why almost no contractor counts?","• What is a mercenary legally (AP I, Art. 47, 1977): Someone meeting six cumulative conditions: specially recruited to fight; directly takes part in hostilities; motivated by private gain and paid much more than regular soldiers; not a national or resident of a party; not a member of a party's armed forces; not sent officially by another state.\n• Why does almost no contractor count as a mercenary: All six conditions must be met. This is the core legal gap."],
  ["pmsc","Inherently state functions, and PMSC vs mercenary vs state deployment?","• What are \"inherently state functions\": Tasks that should never be outsourced, above all direct participation in combat and detention. Where to draw the line is a central debate.\n• PMSC vs mercenary vs state deployment: A PMSC is a company; a mercenary is a legal status; a state-to-state deployment (Rwanda in Mozambique) is lawful when the host consents."],
  ["pmsc","Scale of the PMSC industry?","In Iraq and Afghanistan contractors at times outnumbered US troops; worth hundreds of billions of dollars a year."],
  ["pmsc","The PMSC accountability gap, and Wagner in Africa?","• The accountability gap: Contractors fall between national jurisdictions and the law of armed conflict; victims rarely get remedies.\n• Wagner in Africa: where and when: Central African Republic (from 2018), Sudan, Libya (2019–20), Mozambique (2019), Mali (from 2021)."],
  ["pmsc","Landmark abuse: Nisour Square?","Blackwater, Baghdad 2007: 17 Iraqi civilians killed."],
  ["pmsc","Landmark abuse: Moura, Mali?","March 2022, about 500 killed. The UN human rights office implicated Malian troops and \"foreign military personnel\", widely reported as Wagner."],
  ["pmsc","What happened to Wagner's African operations?","After Prigozhin's death (August 2023) they were folded into Russia's state-run Africa Corps. Wagner left Mali in 2025; Africa Corps stayed."],
  ["pmsc","What do Wagner-style deals often trade?","Security for mining concessions."],
  ["pmsc","Does the UN itself use private security?","Yes, to guard premises, under its 2012 guidelines."],
  ["pmsc","OAU Mercenary Convention, AP I Art. 47 and the 1989 Convention?","• OAU Convention for the Elimination of Mercenarism in Africa: 1977 (Libreville; in force 1985). Binding, regional; written against Cold War \"soldiers of fortune\"; does not fit modern companies.\n• Additional Protocol I, Art. 47: 1977. Binding; mercenaries lose combatant and POW status. Definition too narrow.\n• 1989 International Convention against Mercenaries: GA res. 44/34, in force 2001. Binding, but ratified by only a few dozen states and no major military power. Mozambique is not a party."],
  ["pmsc","Montreux Document and the ICoC?","• The Montreux Document: 17 September 2008 (Switzerland and the ICRC). Non-binding; restates states' legal obligations and lists about 70 good practices. Supported by about 60 states plus the EU, OSCE and NATO. Mozambique has not joined.\n• International Code of Conduct (ICoC): 2010; oversight body ICoCA created 2013 (Geneva). Voluntary industry self-regulation; companies sign on and are certified."],
  ["pmsc","HRC working groups on mercenaries and on PMSCs?","• HRC Working Group on the use of mercenaries: Created 2005 as successor to a Special Rapporteur (from 1987).\n• HRC intergovernmental working group on PMSCs: 2010 (res. 15/26); new mandate 2017 (res. 36/11). Drafting the content of an international regulatory framework; seventh session in Geneva, April 2026. Whether it should be binding is still disputed."],
  ["pmsc","The three camps in the PMSC debate?","• Camp 1 in the PMSC debate: binding convention: African Group (South Africa drafted early texts), many G77 states, the UN Working Group. Voluntary codes have failed; victims need remedies; home states of companies need binding rules.\n• Camp 2: non-binding plus national law: US, UK, many EU states, the industry. Montreux and ICoC work; a treaty would be slow and hard to enforce; regulate through licensing and contracts.\n• Camp 3: sovereignty first: Russia, some host governments. States may choose their security partners; criticism of Wagner or Africa Corps is political."],
  ["pmsc","A/RES/80/194: what it does and why Western states vote no?","• A/RES/80/194: 15 December 2025 (also 79/162 of 2024): the annual Third Committee resolution on the use of mercenaries as a means of violating human rights and impeding self-determination. Adopted 121–53–5; Mozambique yes.\n• What does that annual resolution do: Condemns mercenary activity, urges states to ratify the 1989 Convention, renews the Working Group's work including on PMSCs.\n• Why do Western states vote no on it: It frames the issue under self-determination and mixes mercenaries with lawful companies (the EU says the two \"should not be confused\")."],
  ["pmsc","Working Group on mercenaries report (August 2025)?","\"Role of mercenaries, mercenary-related actors and PMSCs as proxies, enablers and facilitators\": how states use them to fight without accountability."],
  ["pmsc","HRC resolution 51/13 (2022)?","Most recent renewal of the Working Group on mercenaries' mandate."],
  ["pmsc","Montreux Document UN reference?","A/63/467–S/2008/636; circulated to the GA and Security Council."],
  ["pmsc","Security Council resolution 2719 (2023)?","Framework for UN-assessed funding of AU-led peace operations: an alternative to hiring contractors."],
  ["pmsc","Which Council panel reports documented Wagner abuses?","Sanctions panel reports on the Central African Republic, Libya and Mali."],
  ["pmsc","OHCHR report on Moura (May 2023)?","About 500 killed in March 2022; implicated Malian troops and foreign military personnel."],
  ["pmsc","Amnesty's March 2021 report on Cabo Delgado?","\"What I Saw Is Death\": war crimes by insurgents, Mozambican forces and Dyck Advisory Group, based on 79 interviews with displaced people."],
  ["pmsc","Wagner and Dyck Advisory Group in Cabo Delgado?","• Wagner in Cabo Delgado: From September 2019, about 200 Russian contractors with helicopters. They did not know the terrain, clashed with Mozambican troops, suffered ambushes and pulled back within months.\n• Dyck Advisory Group in Cabo Delgado: South African company founded by former Zimbabwean colonel Lionel Dyck; helicopter gunships; April 2020 to April 2021. Amnesty accused it of firing indiscriminately into crowds."],
  ["pmsc","Other private firms in Mozambique?","Paramount and Burnham Global supplied equipment and training."],
  ["pmsc","The shift from contractors to states, and the lesson Mozambique draws?","• The shift from contractors to states: After the Palma attack Mozambique turned to Rwanda (July 2021, bilateral) and SADC's SAMIM (July 2021 to July 2024), with EU training. They retook Mocímboa da Praia in August 2021.\n• The lesson Mozambique draws on contractors: Contractors failed militarily and left an accountability gap; state and regional deployments under clear mandates worked better. Contractors are a symptom of weak state capacity; the answer is capable, accountable national forces and regional cooperation."],
  ["pmsc","Mozambique's PMSC position: framework, limits, home states, remedies, capacity, sovereignty?","• Mozambique's PMSC position: the framework: A legally binding international framework through the HRC working group, with GA1 giving political backing.\n• Mozambique's PMSC position: limits: No outsourcing of direct participation in hostilities or detention.\n• Mozambique's PMSC position: home-state responsibility: Countries where PMSCs are registered must license, vet and prosecute them, so host states are not left alone.\n• Mozambique's PMSC position: remedies: Remedies for victims, including in the country where the harm happened.\n• Mozambique's PMSC position: capacity: Security sector reform so states do not need contractors: training, funding (AU–UN financing model, S/RES/2719) and regional forces like SAMIM.\n• Mozambique's PMSC position: sovereignty: States may seek lawful help, but rules apply equally to all providers, Russian, Western or any other."],
  ["pmsc","PMSC resolution ideas?","• PMSC resolution idea 1: Call on the HRC working group to finalise a draft binding instrument by a set date.\n• PMSC resolution idea 2: Encourage states to join the Montreux Document and require ICoCA membership in all contracts as an interim step (Mozambique could announce it is considering joining).\n• PMSC resolution idea 3: A voluntary UN registry of PMSCs and contracts, run by UNODA or OHCHR.\n• PMSC resolution idea 4: Model national legislation and licensing, with UNREC training for African states.\n• PMSC resolution idea 5: Update the 1977 OAU Convention through the African Union."],
  ["pmsc","PMSC tough questions: Wagner, Montreux, Rwanda, GA1 vs HRC?","• \"Mozambique hired Wagner. Why should anyone listen?\": Because we learned the hard way. We have seen what happens without rules, which is exactly why we want them. Own it; don't dodge.\n• \"Mozambique hasn't joined Montreux or the 1989 Convention.\": Acknowledge it: both have gaps (non-binding; outdated definition), which is why Mozambique wants one modern framework, and joining Montreux is a reasonable step meanwhile.\n• \"Is Rwanda's deployment any different from Wagner?\": Yes: state-to-state, at Mozambique's request, by a state's armed forces under its chain of command and accountable under international law. Contractors answer to a client and shareholders.\n• \"What can GA1 do if the treaty is negotiated in the HRC?\": Give political direction, ask for SG reports, connect the human rights and security tracks, and build the majority a treaty needs."],
  ["laws","LAWS: is there a definition, the ICRC definition, automatic vs autonomous?","• Is there an agreed definition of LAWS: No.\n• ICRC working definition of autonomous weapons: Weapons that select and apply force to targets without human intervention: after a person activates them, they strike based on sensor input matching a \"target profile\".\n• Automatic vs autonomous: Landmines and defensive systems like Phalanx or Iron Dome react automatically in narrow situations; the concern is systems that search for and choose targets across time and space, increasingly with AI."],
  ["laws","US definition of autonomous weapons?","Directive 3000.09 (updated 2023): once activated, can select and engage targets without further human intervention."],
  ["laws","Human in, on, or out of the loop?","In: a human decides each strike. On: a human supervises and can stop it. Out: no human involvement."],
  ["laws","What is meaningful human control?","People must keep enough understanding, judgement and control over a weapon to be responsible for its effects. Also no agreed definition; the heart of the topic."],
  ["laws","LAWS concerns: IHL, accountability, dignity, proliferation, escalation, bias?","• LAWS and IHL: what is the question: Can a machine apply distinction, proportionality and precaution? The Martens Clause adds \"principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience\".\n• The accountability gap for LAWS: If a machine kills unlawfully, who is responsible: commander, programmer or manufacturer?\n• Human dignity argument: A machine should not decide who lives or dies.\n• Proliferation concern: Cheap drones and software spread to armed groups; Islamic State affiliates already use commercial drones.\n• Escalation concern: Lower threshold for war and fast escalation when no soldiers are at risk.\n• Bias and error concern: Algorithms trained on limited data may misidentify people; darker-skinned people and the Global South most at risk."],
  ["laws","Real-world use: Libya?","UN Panel of Experts (S/2021/229): Turkish-made Kargu-2 drones \"hunted down\" retreating forces in 2020, possibly without a human link."],
  ["laws","Other reported AI-assisted targeting?","Ukraine, Gaza and, in 2026, Iran."],
  ["laws","LAWS timeline 2013–2019?","• LAWS timeline: 2013: Special Rapporteur Christof Heyns reports to the HRC, calls for moratoria. Campaign to Stop Killer Robots launches.\n• LAWS timeline: 2014–2017: 2014–16 informal expert meetings under the CCW in Geneva. 2017: the CCW creates a Group of Governmental Experts on LAWS.\n• LAWS timeline: 2019: GGE agrees 11 Guiding Principles (IHL applies fully; human responsibility cannot be transferred to machines; accountability; risk assessment)."],
  ["laws","LAWS timeline 2023–2025?","• LAWS timeline: 2023: Belén Communiqué (Latin America and Caribbean, February). GGE mandate to 2026 to develop \"a set of elements of an instrument, without prejudging its nature\". US Political Declaration on Responsible Military Use of AI and Autonomy. SG and ICRC President call for a binding instrument by 2026 (October). A/RES/78/241 (22 December; 152–4–11) requests an SG report.\n• LAWS timeline: 2024: Freetown Communiqué (ECOWAS, April) calls for urgent binding negotiations. Vienna conference \"Humanity at the Crossroads\" (April). SG report A/79/88. A/RES/79/62 (2 December; 166–3–15) creates informal consultations in New York.\n• LAWS timeline: 2025: Informal consultations in New York (May). A/RES/80/57 (1 December; 164–6–7; Mozambique yes)."],
  ["laws","Who voted against and who abstained on A/RES/80/57?","• Who voted against A/RES/80/57: Belarus, Burundi, North Korea, Israel, Russia, the US.\n• Who abstained on A/RES/80/57: Argentina, China, Iran, Nicaragua, Poland, Saudi Arabia, Türkiye."],
  ["laws","Campaigners' view of A/RES/80/57?","Disappointing because it did not launch negotiations."],
  ["laws","LAWS 2026 and the CCW Review Conference (16–20 Nov 2026)?","• LAWS timeline: 2026: The GGE worked through the Chair's rolling text. At GA1's opening (6 October) the High Representative cited \"agreed elements for an instrument on LAWS\" as a recent success. The NPT Review Conference failed again.\n• CCW Seventh Review Conference: 16–20 November 2026 in Geneva: decides whether to give the GGE a mandate to negotiate an instrument, based on the rolling text. One week after your interview: mention it."],
  ["laws","The two-tier approach?","Prohibit systems that cannot be used in line with IHL or operate without meaningful human control; regulate all others with limits on targets, duration, area and context, human supervision, ability to deactivate, testing and legal reviews (AP I Art. 36)."],
  ["laws","The four camps on LAWS?","• Camp: binding treaty now: Austria (leads GA resolutions), Mexico, Costa Rica, Brazil, Sierra Leone and ECOWAS, the African Group, most of Latin America, the Pacific. Negotiate a binding instrument, in the CCW or, if blocked, in the GA.\n• Camp: CCW only, by consensus: Many European states, Japan, South Korea, Australia. Support an instrument but in the CCW, where all major military powers take part.\n• Camp: no new law: US, Russia, India, Israel. Existing IHL is enough; prefer non-binding principles and national reviews. Russia says it is premature.\n• Camp: narrow ban: China: ban \"unacceptable\" fully autonomous systems under a very narrow definition; abstained on A/RES/80/57."],
  ["laws","Mozambique on LAWS: votes, its own words, African Group position?","• Mozambique on LAWS: votes and groups: Voted yes on A/RES/80/57; aligns with African Group, NAM and G77 statements.\n• Mozambique's own words on autonomy (23 Oct 2025): \"A dangerous convergence between conventional weapons and emerging technologies, including autonomous systems, armed drones, and AI-enabled targeting\"; \"the need to preserve human control and accountability over all uses of force\".\n• African Group position on LAWS: A new legally binding instrument with prohibitions and regulations ensuring meaningful human control over the use of force."],
  ["laws","Mozambique on 22 Oct 2025?","Called for \"algorithmic accountability and AI safeguards\" to \"prevent the automation of destruction\"."],
  ["laws","Mozambique's four angles on LAWS?","• Mozambique angle: insurgency: It fears these weapons spreading to non-state armed groups.\n• Mozambique angle: developing country: No Africa as testing ground (cite Libya 2020); inclusive talks, not rules written only by producer states.\n• Mozambique angle: landmines: Decades of clearance, mine-free in 2015: \"We know what it costs when weapons act without human judgement.\"\n• Mozambique angle: capacity: International cooperation and capacity building so developing states can take part in technical talks."],
  ["laws","Honest gap on LAWS?","Mozambique is not a party to the CCW. Turn it into a point: this is why Mozambique wants the GA, where all 193 states sit, to stay involved, and it could consider joining the CCW."],
  ["laws","LAWS resolution ideas?","• LAWS resolution idea 1: Urge the CCW Review Conference to adopt a negotiating mandate; if it fails, convene negotiations under the GA.\n• LAWS resolution idea 2: Endorse the two-tier approach and a working definition of meaningful human control.\n• LAWS resolution idea 3: National legal weapons reviews and information sharing, with UNIDIR support.\n• LAWS resolution idea 4: A capacity-building fund so developing states can participate.\n• LAWS resolution idea 5: Measures against proliferation to non-state actors."],
  ["laws","LAWS tough questions: why care, is IHL enough, CCW or GA?","• \"Why does Mozambique care? You don't build these weapons.\": Because we may be where they are used, by states or armed groups like the one in Cabo Delgado. Rules made only by producers protect producers.\n• \"Isn't existing IHL enough?\": IHL applies, but it was written for human decision-makers; the accountability gap and lack of a definition show we need specific rules. Even the 11 Guiding Principles admit human responsibility cannot be transferred to machines.\n• \"CCW or GA?\": Prefer the CCW if it delivers a negotiating mandate in November 2026; if it stays blocked by consensus the GA must act. Mozambique, not a CCW party, has a direct interest in the GA track."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR: name, status, and who is High Commissioner?","• UNHCR full name: Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (the UN Refugee Agency).\n• UNHCR status in the UN system: A subsidiary organ of the GA (Charter Art. 22); a UN programme, not a specialised agency.\n• Who is the High Commissioner: Barham Salih (Iraq), since 1 January 2026; elected by the GA on 18 December 2025 for five years."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR legal basis?","GA res. 319 (IV) (3 Dec 1949) created it; Statute adopted by GA res. 428 (V), 14 Dec 1950; began work 1 January 1951."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR headquarters and presence?","Geneva; global service centres in Budapest and Copenhagen; operations in about 130 countries."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR history from the 1960s to 2025–26?","• UNHCR history: 1960s–70s: Crises spread to Africa and Asia; the 1967 Protocol made refugee law universal; the 1969 OAU Convention widened the definition in Africa.\n• UNHCR history: 1990s: Moved into war zones (Bosnia, Rwanda) and began helping IDPs and returnees. 1992–96: about 1.7 million Mozambicans came home from Malawi and other neighbours, the largest repatriation UNHCR had organised in Africa.\n• UNHCR history: 2003: GA removed the time limit on the mandate.\n• UNHCR history: 2005: Humanitarian reform and the cluster approach gave UNHCR lead roles for IDPs.\n• UNHCR history: 2016–18: New York Declaration (A/RES/71/1, 2016) and the Global Compact on Refugees (2018).\n• UNHCR history: 2025–26: The worst funding crisis in its history; budget and staff cut sharply."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR mandate: protection, durable solutions, supervision?","• UNHCR's mandate: Provide international protection to refugees and seek permanent solutions. Work is \"of an entirely non-political character\", \"humanitarian and social\".\n• Protection function: Access to asylum, no return to danger (non-refoulement), and rights in the host country.\n• The three durable solutions: Voluntary repatriation in safety and dignity; local integration (including naturalisation); resettlement to a third country. Complementary pathways (study, work, family visas) supplement them.\n• Supervision of the Convention: Art. 35 of the 1951 Convention (and Art. II of the Protocol): states must cooperate with UNHCR, which supervises how they apply it."],
  ["unhcr","Who does UNHCR cover beyond refugees, and how does it differ from IOM?","• Who does UNHCR cover beyond refugees: Asylum seekers, returnees, stateless people (GA mandates 1974 and 1995) and IDPs (with the state's consent).\n• UNHCR vs IOM vs human rights bodies: UNHCR: forcibly displaced and stateless people. IOM: all migrants. Human rights bodies: everyone."],
  ["unhcr","Who is excluded from UNHCR's mandate?","Palestine refugees served by UNRWA (Art. 1D)."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR functions: promotion and advice?","Promote ratification and application of refugee conventions; advise governments; issue guidelines (Handbook on Procedures and Criteria for Determining Refugee Status, 1979, updated)."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR functions: status determination, assistance, clusters, tripartite repatriation?","• UNHCR functions: status determination: Register refugees and decide status where states cannot or do not (mandate RSD).\n• UNHCR functions: emergency assistance: Shelter, core relief items, water, cash, health and education, often with partners; camps and settlements, increasingly urban refugees.\n• UNHCR cluster roles: Leads the global Protection Cluster; co-leads Shelter and Camp Coordination and Camp Management for IDPs.\n• Tripartite repatriation agreement: Country of origin, host country and UNHCR; UNHCR also refers refugees for resettlement and works to prevent and reduce statelessness."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR's limits?","Cannot force states to admit refugees, cannot operate without host consent, no enforcement powers, depends almost entirely on voluntary funding."],
  ["unhcr","1951 Convention: key dates?","28 July 1951; in force 1954."],
  ["unhcr","1951 Convention: refugee definition, Arts. 1F, 31, 33, 35 and rights?","• 1951 Convention: refugee definition (Art. 1A(2)): Outside one's country, well-founded fear of persecution for race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group or political opinion.\n• 1951 Convention: Art. 1F: Excludes war criminals and serious non-political criminals.\n• 1951 Convention: Art. 31: No penalties for illegal entry.\n• 1951 Convention: Art. 33: Non-refoulement.\n• 1951 Convention: Art. 35: Cooperation with UNHCR.\n• 1951 Convention: rights it lists: Non-discrimination, religion, courts, work, education, documents."],
  ["unhcr","1967 Protocol and the 1969 OAU Convention?","• 1967 Protocol: Removed the time limit (events before 1951) and the optional geographic limit (Europe). About 149 states are party to the Convention and/or Protocol.\n• 1969 OAU Refugee Convention: Adds people fleeing external aggression, occupation, foreign domination or events seriously disturbing public order; covers group (prima facie) recognition."],
  ["unhcr","Cartagena, Statelessness Conventions, Guiding Principles on IDPs, Kampala Convention?","• Cartagena Declaration (1984): Latin America's non-binding wider definition, including generalised violence.\n• Statelessness Conventions: 1954 (status of stateless persons) and 1961 (reducing statelessness).\n• Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement (1998): 30 non-binding principles for IDPs.\n• Kampala Convention: AU, 2009, in force 2012. First binding regional IDP treaty; Mozambique ratified it."],
  ["unhcr","Non-refoulement in other treaties?","Convention against Torture Art. 3 and the ICCPR, with no exceptions for torture."],
  ["unhcr","New York Declaration (A/RES/71/1, 2016)?","Launched both Global Compacts and the Comprehensive Refugee Response Framework (CRRF)."],
  ["unhcr","Global Compact on Refugees?","A/RES/73/151, December 2018. Non-binding; four objectives."],
  ["unhcr","The four objectives of the Global Compact?","(1) Ease pressures on host countries. (2) Enhance refugee self-reliance. (3) Expand access to third-country solutions. (4) Support conditions in countries of origin for return in safety and dignity."],
  ["unhcr","GA, ECOSOC and oversight bodies over UNHCR?","• GA's role over UNHCR: Created it and can change its mandate; elects the High Commissioner on the SG's nomination; annual omnibus resolution via the Third Committee, which debates the High Commissioner's report.\n• ECOSOC's role over UNHCR: UNHCR reports to the GA through ECOSOC; ECOSOC created ExCom and decides on new members; its Humanitarian Affairs Segment covers UNHCR's work.\n• UNHCR oversight bodies: Inspector General's Office, UN Board of Auditors, Independent Audit and Oversight Committee, Ethics Office, Evaluation Office."],
  ["unhcr","ExCom: creation, members, meetings, functions, observers, Conclusions?","• ExCom: when created: By ECOSOC in 1958 at the GA's request; first met 1959; replaced the UN Refugee Fund Executive Committee.\n• ExCom: how many members: 110 states (2026), up from 108 at the end of 2023 (Angola was 108th). Members must belong to the UN or a specialised agency and show interest in refugee issues; admission takes one to two years.\n• ExCom: when does it meet: Annual plenary every October in Geneva; the 77th session is 12–16 October 2026.\n• ExCom: functions: Advises the High Commissioner; approves the annual programme and budget; authorises emergency appeals; reviews funds and evaluations; adopts Conclusions on International Protection.\n• ExCom observers: Non-member states, UN agencies, the ICRC and NGOs (coordinated by ICVA).\n• ExCom Conclusions: status: Adopted by consensus; not binding but authoritative soft law."],
  ["unhcr","ExCom Conclusion No. 22?","1981: protection in large-scale influx."],
  ["unhcr","ExCom Conclusion No. 44?","1986: detention of refugees and asylum seekers."],
  ["unhcr","What must you check for the NMUN UNHCR simulation?","The background guide: exactly which body you represent (usually a governing body like ExCom) and what it can adopt."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR regional structure?","Seven bureaux. The Southern Africa bureau (Pretoria) closed 1 October 2025; its work moved to a renamed bureau for Eastern and Southern Africa (Nairobi, 25 countries) and to West and Central Africa (Dakar). Others: Americas; Asia and the Pacific; Europe; Middle East and North Africa."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR funding sources?","Almost entirely voluntary contributions; only about 1% from the UN regular budget."],
  ["unhcr","Why does unearmarked funding matter?","Most money is tied to countries or projects; flexible funding can go to neglected crises like Mozambique's."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR strategy: Strategic Directions, Global Refugee Forum and statelessness?","• UNHCR Strategic Directions 2022–2026: Five directions: protect, respond, include, empower, solve.\n• Global Refugee Forum schedule: Every four years: December 2019, December 2023 (over 1,600 pledges), next 2027; high-level officials' meetings in between (December 2025); Support Platforms for specific situations.\n• Statelessness campaigns: #IBelong (2014–2024), followed by the Global Alliance to End Statelessness (2024). About 4.4 million reported stateless; real number much higher."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR's 2018 policy on diversity?","Age, Gender and Diversity Policy, plus accountability to affected people."],
  ["unhcr","Climate and UNHCR?","Strategic Framework for Climate Action (2021). People fleeing disasters are usually not refugees under the 1951 Convention unless persecution or conflict is also involved."],
  ["unhcr","Route-based approach?","UNHCR and IOM approach to mixed movements; relevant to your second topic."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR partners: IOM, UNICEF, WFP, WHO, UNFPA, OCHA, World Bank?","• IOM's relation to UNHCR: \"UN Migration\", in the UN system since 2016; leads on migrants, co-leads camp management for disaster IDPs, partners on mixed movements. In Cabo Delgado its Displacement Tracking Matrix gives IDP numbers.\n• UNICEF, WFP, WHO and UNFPA as partners: UNICEF: children, education, child protection, water. WFP: food and cash (as in Maratane). WHO and UNFPA: health, mental health, sexual and reproductive health.\n• OCHA's role: Coordinates the humanitarian system and cluster approach under the IASC.\n• World Bank and UNHCR: Development financing for host communities (IDA window for hosts and refugees).\n• Other UNHCR partners: ICRC and NGOs (implementing partners get a large share of UNHCR's budget; refugee-led organisations increasingly funded directly); UNRWA (outside mandate); HRC Special Rapporteur on IDPs."],
  ["unhcr","Global displacement figures and where refugees live?","• Global displacement, end of 2025: 117.8 million forcibly displaced, the first fall in a decade (from 123.2 million), about one in 70 people.\n• Where do most refugees live: In low- and middle-income countries next to their own, often for years (protracted situations)."],
  ["unhcr","Displaced by category?","68.7 million IDPs; 41.6 million refugees (including 6 million Palestine refugees under UNRWA), plus asylum seekers and others in need of protection."],
  ["unhcr","Returns in 2025?","14.7 million returned home, the most UNHCR has recorded, largely to Syria and Afghanistan."],
  ["unhcr","2026 displacement from the Iran war?","Over a million forced from their homes; 3.2 million internally displaced in Iran."],
  ["unhcr","Priorities at the 77th ExCom (Oct 2026)?","Protection with far less money; solutions and returns (voluntary, safe, dignified); burden-sharing ahead of the 2027 Forum; resolving protracted situations; inclusion in national systems."],
  ["unhcr","Criticisms of UNHCR: funding, resettlement, returns, externalisation, refoulement, camps, SEA, IDPs?","• Criticism: funding collapse: Donor cuts led by the US forced UNHCR to shed thousands of staff and close the Southern Africa bureau.\n• Criticism: resettlement: The US, historically the largest resettlement country, suspended its refugee admissions in January 2025.\n• Criticism: premature returns: Mass returns to Afghanistan (many forced from Iran and Pakistan) and to Syria after December 2024 raise doubts about voluntariness and safety.\n• Criticism: externalisation: UNHCR criticised the UK–Rwanda plan (scrapped 2024) and raised concerns about Italy–Albania offshore processing.\n• Criticism: refoulement by states: Pushbacks at Europe's borders; in Africa, Tanzania's forced return of over 10,000 Mozambicans in 2021.\n• Criticism: encampment: Long-term camps (Dadaab, Kakuma in Kenya) keep people dependent; the Global Compact pushes self-reliance.\n• Criticism: accountability: A 2002 UNHCR and Save the Children report exposed sexual exploitation of refugee children by aid workers in West Africa, leading to strict UN SEA rules.\n• Criticism: IDP role: Debate over whether UNHCR should do more for IDPs (the majority of the displaced) or stay focused on refugees."],
  ["unhcr","UN organs relevant to UNHCR?","GA Third Committee (annual resolution, High Commissioner's report); Security Council (conflicts, protection of civilians; UNHCR is non-political and takes no orders from it); HRC (migrants, IDPs); SG's 2022 Action Agenda on Internal Displacement; ECOSOC."],
  ["unhcr","Refugees in Mozambique: numbers, INAR and Maratane?","• Refugees and asylum seekers in Mozambique: About 26,000 (2024 ExCom statement), mainly from DR Congo and Burundi, plus Rwanda, Ethiopia and Somalia. Government body: INAR (National Institute for Refugee Support).\n• Maratane: In Nampula; the only formal settlement in Mozambique, though many refugees live in cities. Mozambique asked UNHCR for help moving to an out-of-camp policy."],
  ["unhcr","Mozambique and statelessness?","Completed a national statelessness study with UNHCR and acceded to both statelessness conventions."],
  ["unhcr","UNHCR in Cabo Delgado and Mozambique's reservations?","• UNHCR in Cabo Delgado: Leads protection; appealed for $38.2 million for northern Mozambique in 2026.\n• Mozambique's weak spot on refugee law: Reservations to the 1951 Convention (including Art. 26 on freedom of movement), applied flexibly in practice."],
  ["unhcr","Cyclone Chido and refugees?","December 2024: destroyed shelters for IDPs and refugees alike."],
  ["unhcr","Why did Mozambique choose UNHCR?","\"Country of origin, transit and asylum at the same time. In the 1990s almost two million of our refugees came home with UNHCR's help. Today over a million are displaced by Cabo Delgado and cyclones, we host Great Lakes refugees, and migrants cross our territory on the southern route. As a long-standing ExCom member, which we chaired in 2014–15, we bring all three perspectives.\""],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS: definition, psychosocial, disorder vs distress, task-shifting?","• IASC (2007) definition of MHPSS: \"Any type of local or outside support that aims to protect or promote psychosocial well-being and/or prevent or treat mental disorder.\"\n• What does \"psychosocial\" mean: The link between psychological effects (emotions, thoughts) and social factors (family, community, culture). Most distress after displacement is a normal reaction to abnormal events, not illness.\n• Mental disorder vs distress: Only some people need clinical care; everyone benefits from safety, dignity and community.\n• Task-shifting: Training non-specialists (nurses, community workers, teachers, refugees themselves) to deliver basic psychological help where specialists are scarce."],
  ["mhpss","The IASC intervention pyramid: four layers from bottom to top?","1) Basic services and security (shelter, food, water, safety). 2) Community and family supports. 3) Focused, non-specialised supports (psychological first aid, basic counselling). 4) Specialised services for the few with severe conditions."],
  ["mhpss","Which document contains the pyramid?","IASC Guidelines on MHPSS in Emergency Settings (2007)."],
  ["mhpss","IASC core principles?","Human rights and equity, participation, do no harm, build on existing resources, integrated services, multi-layered support."],
  ["mhpss","Scale of mental health need: prevalence, stages of stress, workforce gap, at-risk groups?","• How common are mental disorders in conflict settings: About one in five (22%) people have depression, anxiety, PTSD, bipolar disorder or schizophrenia (WHO-supported Lancet study, 2019).\n• Three stages of displacement stress: Before flight (violence, loss), during the journey (smuggling, detention, sexual violence), after arrival (uncertainty, poverty, discrimination, no right to work).\n• MHPSS workforce gap: Low-income countries have fewer than two mental health workers per 100,000 people (WHO Mental Health Atlas), against dozens in rich countries.\n• Groups most at risk: Children and adolescents, survivors of sexual and gender-based violence, former child soldiers, people with disabilities."],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS tools: PFA, mhGAP, UNHCR guidance, PM+/Self-Help Plus, Minimum Service Package?","• Psychological First Aid: WHO, 2011: simple, humane support anyone can give in the first days after a crisis.\n• mhGAP Humanitarian Intervention Guide: WHO and UNHCR, 2015: lets general health workers manage common mental health conditions.\n• UNHCR Operational Guidance on MHPSS: 2013: how UNHCR builds MHPSS into all its sectors, not as a separate project.\n• Problem Management Plus and Self-Help Plus: WHO short, low-cost programmes delivered by non-specialists, tested with refugees.\n• IASC MHPSS Minimum Service Package: 2022: a costed list of essential activities for any emergency."],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS in the Global Compact on Refugees and SDGs?","The Compact (2018) explicitly commits states to expand MHPSS for refugees and host communities. SDG target 3.4 promotes mental health and well-being."],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS debates: funding, clinical vs community, integration, digital, self-reliance?","• Debate: funding: MHPSS is usually among the first services cut; with UNHCR about 35% funded in 2026, programmes are shrinking.\n• Debate: clinical vs community: Western trauma models (PTSD diagnosis, one-to-one therapy) versus community-based, culturally grounded support.\n• Debate: integration: Stand-alone projects versus building MHPSS into health, education, protection and national health systems that host communities also use.\n• Debate: digital tools: Apps and tele-counselling reach more people but raise privacy and access concerns.\n• Debate: self-reliance: The right to work and study is itself one of the strongest protections for mental health."],
  ["mhpss","How UNHCR does MHPSS in practice?","• How does UNHCR integrate MHPSS: In two sectors at once: health (identifying and treating conditions in primary care) and community-based protection (rebuilding social support, preventing violence, helping people with disabilities and survivors).\n• Community-based protection in MHPSS: Refugee outreach volunteers, women's and youth groups and refugee-led organisations identify people in distress and refer them.\n• Who gets Psychological First Aid training at UNHCR: All front-line staff, including registration and border staff.\n• What does mhGAP training enable: General doctors and nurses in refugee clinics can treat depression, psychosis, epilepsy and substance use.\n• WHO scalable interventions: where tested: Problem Management Plus, Self-Help Plus and Step-by-Step (digital self-help), tested in randomised trials with Syrian refugees and South Sudanese refugees in Uganda.\n• How does UNHCR monitor MHPSS: Through its health information system, which records mental health consultations in camps. Also staff care for humanitarian worker burnout and trauma."],
  ["mhpss","Special groups for MHPSS and what helps each?","• Special group: children and adolescents: About half of all refugees are children; risks: violence, separation, lost schooling, recruitment. Help: child-friendly spaces, family tracing and reunification, safe schools, parenting support; UNICEF partnership.\n• Special group: survivors of sexual and gender-based violence: Very common in conflict and on routes; stigma prevents help-seeking. Help: confidential, survivor-centred case management linking health, psychosocial and legal help.\n• Special group: former child soldiers and abductees: Trauma plus community rejection. Help: community reintegration, education, livelihoods, cleansing and reconciliation rituals where culturally appropriate.\n• Special group: persons with disabilities: Higher risk of neglect, often hidden. Help: inclusion under the CRPD; ExCom Conclusion No. 110.\n• Special group: older people, LGBTIQ+ refugees, unaccompanied minors: Isolation and discrimination. Help: targeted outreach and safe spaces.\n• Special group: people in detention or long asylum procedures: Detention and uncertainty sharply worsen mental health. Help: alternatives to detention; faster, fair procedures."],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS case studies: Rohingya, Syrians, Uganda, Ukraine, Cabo Delgado?","• Case: Rohingya in Cox's Bazar: About a million people; large community-based MHPSS with Rohingya volunteers as psychosocial workers; food aid cuts have increased distress.\n• Case: Syrian refugees (Türkiye, Lebanon, Jordan): WHO's PM+ and Self-Help Plus were tested here; MHPSS was integrated into national primary health care.\n• Case: Uganda: Progressive refugee policy (freedom of movement, right to work, land for farming) is cited as mental health protection in itself; Self-Help Plus trialled with South Sudanese refugee women.\n• Case: Ukraine: Since 2022 MHPSS is a national priority with a national mental health programme championed by the First Lady.\n• Case: Cabo Delgado: UN agencies and NGOs run mobile psychosocial teams, child-friendly spaces and survivor services in IDP sites, but funding covers only a fraction of need."],
  ["mhpss","WHO Comprehensive Mental Health Action Plan?","2013–2030, adopted by the World Health Assembly; includes humanitarian settings."],
  ["mhpss","WHO Global Action Plan on refugee and migrant health?","2019, extended to 2030 in 2023; the World report on the health of refugees and migrants followed in 2022 (first global report; mental health a major gap)."],
  ["mhpss","CRC Article 39?","States must promote physical and psychological recovery and reintegration of child victims of armed conflict and abuse."],
  ["mhpss","2025 UN high-level meeting on NCDs and mental health?","Governments committed to expand mental health care including in emergencies; first time mental health sat alongside NCDs at this level."],
  ["mhpss","WHA77.3?","May 2024, by consensus (led by Ukraine and the Netherlands): \"Strengthening MHPSS before, during and after armed conflicts, natural and human-caused disasters and health and other emergencies\". Urges community-based MHPSS in preparedness and response including for health and aid workers; WHO reports annually 2025–2031. The single most important text for this topic."],
  ["mhpss","GA annual UNHCR resolution and MHPSS?","Third Committee resolution endorses UNHCR's work including protection, health and community-based support."],
  ["mhpss","Binding duties on psychosocial recovery?","CRC Art. 39 (recovery of child victims) and the CRPD (2006) on persons with psychosocial disabilities."],
  ["mhpss","Mozambique's MHPSS angles: Cabo Delgado, capacity, post-war reintegration, host communities?","• Mozambique angle: Cabo Delgado trauma: Beheadings, burned villages, child abductions (some forced to fight), sexual violence, repeated displacement, then cyclones. About 80% of the newly displaced are women and children.\n• Mozambique angle: capacity: Specialists are very scarce, so it relies on community-based care and task-shifting (mid-level psychiatric technicians, trained community workers).\n• Mozambique angle: post-civil-war experience: Communities reintegrated former child soldiers partly through traditional cleansing and reconciliation ceremonies plus family reunification: a well-known example of culturally grounded psychosocial recovery.\n• Mozambique angle: host communities: In Cabo Delgado and Nampula they are as poor as the displaced, so services must serve both."],
  ["mhpss","Mozambique's MHPSS position?","• Mozambique position: MHPSS status: Life-saving, not optional; should get a protected share of humanitarian appeals.\n• Mozambique position: approach: Community-based and culturally grounded, following the IASC pyramid; train teachers, health workers, religious leaders and displaced people themselves.\n• Mozambique position: integration: Into national primary health care, serving refugees, IDPs and hosts, and lasting after the emergency.\n• Mozambique position: priority groups: Children and survivors of sexual violence: child-friendly spaces, reintegration of abducted children, safe schools.\n• Mozambique position: burden-sharing: Host and conflict-affected countries carry the load; donors must fund predictably, including through 2027 Global Refugee Forum pledges.\n• Mozambique position: IDPs: Apply it equally to IDPs, who are the majority of the displaced."],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS resolution ideas?","• MHPSS resolution idea 1: A minimum MHPSS funding share in every UNHCR appeal.\n• MHPSS resolution idea 2: Scale up WHO and UNHCR non-specialist programmes by training community workers.\n• MHPSS resolution idea 3: MHPSS pledges at the 2027 Global Refugee Forum.\n• MHPSS resolution idea 4: Include mental health indicators in UNHCR needs assessments.\n• MHPSS resolution idea 5: Protect refugees' right to work and study as a mental health measure."],
  ["mhpss","MHPSS tough questions?","• \"With UNHCR a third funded, why mental health over food and shelter?\": They are not alternatives: the pyramid starts with basic services delivered with dignity, and community-based MHPSS is cheap. Untreated trauma undermines everything else, from learning to work to return.\n• \"Aren't Western trauma models unsuitable in Africa?\": That is Mozambique's point: support must be community-based and culturally grounded, as in our reintegration of child soldiers after the civil war, while still offering clinical care to the few who need it.\n• \"Does Mozambique have the capacity?\": Not alone. Very few specialists, so it relies on task-shifting, and international cooperation and predictable funding matter.\n• \"What can UNHCR do that WHO can't?\": UNHCR is in camps, settlements and border points and can build MHPSS into protection, registration and community work; WHO sets clinical standards."],
  ["routes","Mixed movements; refugee vs migrant vs asylum seeker; smuggling vs trafficking?","• Mixed movements: Refugees, asylum seekers and migrants travelling together on the same routes, with the same smugglers and dangers, but with different legal rights.\n• Refugee vs migrant vs asylum seeker: Refugee: protected by the 1951 Convention, must not be returned to danger. Migrant: moves for other reasons but still has human rights. Asylum seeker: claim not yet decided.\n• Smuggling vs trafficking: Palermo Protocols (2000). Smuggling: paid service to cross a border illegally, a crime against the state, ends at the border. Trafficking: exploitation by force, fraud or coercion, a crime against the person, possible without crossing a border."],
  ["routes","Non-refoulement, pushback, externalisation, route-based approach?","• Non-refoulement: scope: Ban on returning anyone to persecution or serious harm (Refugee Convention Art. 33; CAT Art. 3). Customary international law, so binds even non-parties, and applies at the border.\n• Pushback: Forcing people back across a border without assessing claims; a breach of non-refoulement.\n• Externalisation: Rich states paying or partnering with transit states to stop people before they arrive.\n• Route-based approach: UNHCR's (with IOM) strategy of offering protection, information and alternatives all along a route: origin, transit and destination countries."],
  ["routes","Migration routes and dangers: Central Mediterranean, Atlantic, Sahara, Eastern, Southern?","• Central Mediterranean route: Libya and Tunisia to Italy. Deadliest sea route; tens of thousands dead or missing since 2014 (IOM). 2023 Pylos shipwreck off Greece killed about 600. Detention and abuse in Libya.\n• Atlantic route: West Africa to the Canary Islands; surged in 2023–24; long, deadly open-boat journeys.\n• Sahara crossings: UNHCR, IOM and the Mixed Migration Centre warn more people may die crossing the desert than the sea.\n• Eastern route: Horn of Africa to Yemen and Saudi Arabia; sinkings off Yemen; HRW 2023 accused Saudi border guards of killing migrants.\n• Southern route: Horn of Africa and Great Lakes to South Africa via Kenya, Tanzania, Malawi, Mozambique, Zambia, Zimbabwe; almost entirely smuggler-run and understudied. Your route."],
  ["routes","Darién Gap?","Colombia to Panama; over 500,000 crossings in 2023, collapsed in 2025 after US and Panamanian policy changes."],
  ["routes","Andaman Sea and Bay of Bengal?","Rohingya boat journeys from Myanmar and Bangladesh."],
  ["routes","Law of the sea duty on rescue?","Duty to rescue people in distress: UNCLOS Art. 98, SOLAS and SAR conventions."],
  ["routes","Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration?","2018, alongside the Refugee Compact. Objective 8 commits states to save lives and look for missing migrants."],
  ["routes","Migration Compact objectives 8, 9, 10, and the debate on externalisation?","• Migration Compact Objectives 8, 9, 10: 8: save lives and coordinate on missing migrants. 9: counter smuggling. 10: prevent trafficking.\n• Debate: externalisation: Does it save lives or just move the danger to Libya or the Sahara?"],
  ["routes","Compact references?","Global Compact for Migration: A/RES/73/195 (December 2018); Progress Declaration of the first International Migration Review Forum: A/RES/76/266 (2022)."],
  ["routes","EU Pact on Migration and Asylum?","Adopted 2024, applying from June 2026."],
  ["routes","Externalisation deals to know?","EU–Türkiye (2016), Italy–Libya (2017), EU–Tunisia (2023), Italy–Albania (2023). The UK–Rwanda plan was scrapped in 2024."],
  ["routes","Debates: borders vs asylum, search and rescue, burden-sharing?","• Debate: border control vs asylum: States' right to control borders against the duty not to push people back.\n• Debate: search and rescue: State-run rescue versus criminalising NGO rescue ships.\n• Debate: burden-sharing: Transit states in the Global South carry the risk while funding falls."],
  ["routes","Debate: safe and legal pathways?","Resettlement, humanitarian visas, family reunification, labour mobility as the real alternative to smugglers."],
  ["routes","Missing migrants figures and the \"no one cares if you live or die\" report?","• IOM Missing Migrants Project figures: 2024 deadliest year on record (about 9,200 dead or missing after revision); 7,904 in 2025 (aid cuts also reduced data); over 80,000 since 2014. Real numbers higher.\n• \"On this journey, no one cares if you live or die\": UNHCR, IOM and Mixed Migration Centre (vol. 1 2020, vol. 2 July 2024): torture, kidnapping for ransom, sexual violence, trafficking, organ removal, expulsions; Sahara deaths likely about twice those at sea; near-total impunity. Over 31,000 interviews."],
  ["routes","Security Council resolution 2240?","2015, renewed yearly: authorised inspection of vessels suspected of migrant smuggling off Libya, while insisting on refugees' and migrants' rights."],
  ["routes","UNHCR 10-Point Plan of Action?","On Refugee Protection and Mixed Migration (2006, updated 2016): the classic toolkit: data, protection-sensitive entry, referral, solutions."],
  ["routes","ExCom Conclusion No. 58?","1989: refugees and asylum seekers who move irregularly from a first country of asylum."],
  ["routes","HRW \"They Fired On Us Like Rain\" (2023)?","Saudi border guards accused of killing migrants on the Eastern route."],
  ["routes","GA annual resolutions on migrants?","\"Protection of migrants\" and \"Office of the UNHCR\" (Third Committee): protect migrants' rights and access to asylum regardless of status."],
  ["routes","Southern route through Mozambique and the Tete tragedy?","• The southern route through Mozambique: Migrants from Ethiopia and Somalia and refugees from the Great Lakes go through Tanzania and Malawi, cross into Mozambique (often Tete or Niassa) and head for South Africa. IOM recorded about 33,700 movements from Mozambique to South Africa in 2025, mostly Mozambicans and Malawians.\n• The Tete tragedy: 24 March 2020: police found 64 Ethiopian men dead from suffocation in a sealed container from Malawi; 14 survived. The driver admitted being paid to smuggle them."],
  ["routes","Similar finds on the southern route?","A mass grave of Ethiopian migrants in northern Malawi (2022); bodies dumped near Lusaka, Zambia (2022)."],
  ["routes","Mozambicans as asylum seekers?","People fleeing Cabo Delgado who tried to reach Tanzania: over 10,000 forcibly returned in 2021 via Negomano. UNHCR called it refoulement; Mozambique knows from its own citizens why non-refoulement matters."],
  ["routes","Other Mozambique migration concerns?","Deportations and xenophobic violence in South Africa; a 2008 anti-trafficking law; cooperation with IOM and SADC."],
  ["routes","Mozambique's position on migration routes?","• Mozambique position: non-refoulement: Absolute, at borders and at sea; speaks from the 2021 experience of its citizens being pushed back.\n• Mozambique position: smugglers: Protect people, prosecute smugglers: target smuggling and trafficking networks, never the people they move; identify trafficking victims and refugees early.\n• Mozambique position: regional cooperation: Through SADC and the AU: shared data, joint training of border officials, the Southern African migration dialogue (MIDSA), cooperation with IOM and UNHCR.\n• Mozambique position: border management: Protection-sensitive: screening and referral to asylum at border posts, not only security checks.\n• Mozambique position: pathways: Safe and legal pathways and labour mobility in southern Africa as alternatives to smugglers.\n• Mozambique position: burden-sharing: Transit and host states in Africa need predictable funding; closure of UNHCR's Southern Africa bureau shows the cost of donor cuts.\n• Mozambique position: African definitions: Applies the OAU Convention's wider refugee definition and the Kampala Convention."],
  ["routes","Routes resolution ideas?","• Routes resolution idea 1: Expand UNHCR's route-based approach to the southern route, with protection points in Malawi, Mozambique and Zimbabwe.\n• Routes resolution idea 2: A regional SADC protocol on search for missing migrants and identification of the dead.\n• Routes resolution idea 3: Training and referral systems at border posts, funded through the Global Compact.\n• Routes resolution idea 4: More resettlement and complementary pathways from transit countries.\n• Routes resolution idea 5: Independent monitoring of pushbacks."],
  ["routes","Routes tough questions?","• \"Mozambique has reservations to the Refugee Convention. Isn't that hypocritical?\": In practice it applies them flexibly and many refugees live in cities; it is reviewing its framework with UNHCR, adopted an IDP policy and ratified the Kampala Convention. Concede, then pivot.\n• \"Should states be allowed to stop migrants at borders?\": States can manage borders, but every person must be able to ask for asylum and must not be pushed back to danger.\n• \"What should UNHCR do about smuggling? It isn't a police agency.\": UNHCR identifies and protects refugees within mixed flows, informs about risks and advocates for pathways; law enforcement belongs to UNODC and states."]
];
